/* Purview Gurukul — RCM Basics course content.
   Rebuilt from the Purview "3-Day RCM" induction deck (2017), reorganised into
   modules and lessons, with outdated figures and rules brought current.
   Each lesson: line (one-line answer), body (paragraphs, **bold** allowed),
   terms (glossary pairs), flow (numbered process, hands off to `to`),
   compare (table), tips ("on the job"), remember (callout), step (RCM step #). */
"use strict";
const BASICS_MODULES=[
{id:"m0",short:"Working with the US",title:"Working with the US",day:"Day 1",
 blurb:"Orientation for a team in India serving US providers and payers: the country, the phone, and the words you must spell correctly.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"us-culture",title:"The US in twenty points",
  line:"You will spend your day talking to Americans. Knowing how the country works, what it values and when it is closed makes those calls easier.",
  body:[
   "**Society and values.** The United States is a melting pot of ethnic, racial and religious groups, with Christianity the largest faith and American English the dominant language, spoken in many regional dialects. It is a highly individualistic society that values personal freedom, self-expression and direct communication. It has a strong tradition of democracy and individual rights, and a history of political and social activism: the civil rights movement, feminism, LGBTQ+ rights.",
   "**Work.** Americans are known for an entrepreneurial, innovative spirit (Silicon Valley), a strong work ethic with long hours, and a focus on productivity, success and achievement. Business is informal in tone but precise about time: a 10:00 call starts at 10:00. Payer and provider offices run roughly 8 am to 5 pm in their own time zone, Monday to Friday.",
   "**Everyday culture.** Sports (NFL, NBA, MLB) and Hollywood are constant conversation topics. Regional food (Tex-Mex, Southern barbecue, New England clam chowder) sits alongside a love of fast food (McDonald's, Burger King, KFC). Music runs from jazz and blues to rock and roll, hip hop and country. The country prizes its universities (Harvard, Yale, MIT), its cars, its consumer culture and its landscapes, from the Rocky Mountains to the Grand Canyon and Hawaii.",
   "**Military culture.** Military service and veterans are deeply respected. That matters for us: TRICARE, the VA and CHAMPVA are payers, and \"thank you for your service\" is normal when a patient or caller mentions serving.",
   "**Holidays close offices.** Payers and provider offices close on federal holidays: New Year's Day, Martin Luther King Jr. Day (3rd Monday of January), Presidents' Day (3rd Monday of February), Memorial Day (last Monday of May), Juneteenth (19 June), Independence Day (4 July), Labor Day (1st Monday of September), Columbus Day (2nd Monday of October), Veterans Day (11 November), Thanksgiving (4th Thursday of November, and usually the Friday after) and Christmas Day. Plan call blocks around them."
  ],
  compare:{head:["US time zone","Abbreviation","Behind IST by","When it is 9 am there, IST is"],rows:[["Eastern","ET (EST/EDT)","9.5 h (10.5 h in winter)","6:30 pm (7:30 pm)"],["Central","CT","10.5 h (11.5 h)","7:30 pm (8:30 pm)"],["Mountain","MT","11.5 h (12.5 h)","8:30 pm (9:30 pm)"],["Pacific","PT","12.5 h (13.5 h)","9:30 pm (10:30 pm)"]]},
  terms:[["Federal holiday","A nationwide public holiday when government offices, banks and most payers close."],["Daylight saving time","Most US states move clocks forward one hour from the second Sunday of March to the first Sunday of November, so the gap to India shrinks by an hour in summer."],["Business day","Monday to Friday, excluding holidays. Timely filing and appeal deadlines are usually counted in calendar days, but payer turnaround times are quoted in business days."]],
  tips:["Dates are written month/day/year in the US: 03/04/2026 is 4 March, not 3 April. Every DOS you read or type follows this.","Money is written with a period for decimals and a comma for thousands: $1,736.00.","Names are first name then last name. The last name is the family name; payers index members by last name and DOB."],
  remember:"When you call a payer at 7 pm IST, it is morning on the US East Coast. Pacific-coast payers only open around 9:30 pm IST."},
 {id:"us-fun-facts",title:"US fun facts for the break room",
  line:"Ten pieces of trivia from the induction deck. Useful for small talk, and a reminder that the country you bill in has its own stories.",
  body:[
   "The shortest war in history was between the United States and Zanzibar on 27 August 1896; it lasted about 38 minutes. The Great Seal on the back of the one-dollar bill shows an unfinished pyramid, meant to say the country is still growing. More than 300 languages are spoken in the US, English the most widely.",
   "The potato chip was invented in 1853 by chef George Crum in Saratoga Springs, New York. The current 50-star flag was designed by a 17-year-old student, Robert G. Heft, as a school project that earned a B minus before it was adopted in 1960. The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France, dedicated in 1886.",
   "George Washington was not the Continental Congress's first idea for president; John Hancock was approached and declined. The world's first roller coaster opened in 1884 at Coney Island, New York. The US has the world's largest economy, with GDP now above $29 trillion, and a long list of inventions to its name: the light bulb, the telephone, the airplane and the internet."
  ],
  remember:"Small talk is fine on a call, but keep it to a sentence. Reps are measured on handle time just like you are."},
 {id:"phonetic-alphabet",title:"The phonetic alphabet",
  line:"Member IDs, claim numbers and names are read letter by letter on payer calls. Use the NATO alphabet so B is never heard as D.",
  body:[
   "Every payer call involves spelling: the patient's last name, the member ID, the provider NPI, your call reference number. US reps use the NATO phonetic alphabet, and so should you. Say the letter, then the word: \"W as in Whiskey, 1, 2, 3, 4\". Read numbers in groups of three or four, and repeat the whole string back before you hang up.",
   "Letters that are commonly confused on the phone: B/D/P/T/V, M/N, S/F, and the vowels A/E/I. Zero is read \"zero\", never \"oh\", because O is also a letter in many IDs."
  ],
  compare:{head:["A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H","I","J","K","L","M"],rows:[["Alpha","Bravo","Charlie","Delta","Echo","Foxtrot","Golf","Hotel","India","Juliet","Kilo","Lima","Mike"],["N","O","P","Q","R","S","T","U","V","W","X","Y","Z"],["November","Oscar","Papa","Quebec","Romeo","Sierra","Tango","Uniform","Victor","Whiskey","X-ray","Yankee","Zulu"]]},
  tips:["Always confirm the claim number and call reference number by reading them back phonetically; it is the proof that the call happened.","When a rep spells something to you, write it as they spell it and repeat it once. Do not guess a letter you did not hear."],
  remember:"Call reference number plus the rep's name plus the date is the minimum documentation for every call note."}
]},
{id:"m1",short:"Healthcare & 3Ps",title:"Healthcare and the 3Ps",day:"Day 1",
 blurb:"How a doctor's visit turns into money in the US, and who the three parties are.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"us-vs-india",title:"How a visit gets paid: India vs the US",
  line:"In India the patient usually pays the doctor and claims from the insurer. In the US the provider bills the insurer, so a medical billing organisation sits between the two.",
  body:[
   "**In India**, when an insured patient visits a doctor, the sequence is: patient visits the doctor's office, receives treatment, pays the doctor, then claims the money back from the insurance company, which reimburses the patient. The doctor is paid immediately and never deals with the insurer.",
   "**In the US**, the patient books an appointment and is treated, but the patient's details go to a **Medical Billing Organisation (MBO)**. The MBO prepares and submits the claim to the insurance company, and the insurer reimburses the doctor directly. The patient pays only their own share (copay, deductible, coinsurance).",
   "Everything in revenue cycle management exists because of that second flow: somebody has to prove to the insurer what was done, why it was done, and that the patient was covered on that date. That somebody is the billing team, and Purview is an MBO."
  ],
  terms:[["Provider","The person or organisation that delivers care and expects to be paid for it."],
   ["Medical Billing Organisation (MBO)","A company that handles coding, claims, payment posting and follow-up on behalf of providers. Also called a billing company or RCM vendor."],
   ["Reimbursement","Payment made by an insurer for a covered service, after the claim is adjudicated."]],
  flow:{to:"The RCM cycle",steps:["Patient books an appointment","Doctor treats the patient (the encounter)","Provider sends patient and visit details to the MBO","MBO codes the visit and submits the claim to the insurance company","Insurance adjudicates and reimburses the provider","Patient is billed for any remaining responsibility"]},
  remember:"In the US the provider carries the credit risk. If the claim is wrong, the provider is the one who waits or goes unpaid."},
 {id:"why-insurance",title:"Why the US needs health insurance",
  line:"US healthcare is the most expensive in the world and there is no universal government coverage, so almost every patient pays through an insurance plan, and almost every provider is paid by one.",
  body:[
   "**Cost.** The US spends about 17% of GDP on healthcare, over $13,000 per person per year. A hospital day can cost thousands of dollars, an ER visit over $2,000, and a surgery tens of thousands. Few families can pay those bills from savings, so coverage is essential, and medical debt is a leading cause of personal bankruptcy.",
   "**No single national system.** Unlike India's mix of out-of-pocket payment, government hospitals and reimbursement policies, or the UK's NHS, the US has a patchwork: employer-sponsored plans for most working-age people, Medicare for the elderly and disabled, Medicaid for low-income people, TRICARE and the VA for the military, Marketplace plans for individuals, and about 8% of people uninsured.",
   "**Four points about insurance in US healthcare.** One: the payer, not the patient, is the provider's main customer for payment, so the claim must satisfy the payer's rules. Two: coverage is tied to employment for most adults, so a job change means a plan change and a new eligibility check. Three: cost sharing (deductibles, coinsurance, copays) is the patient's part and is rising, so patient collections are a real revenue stream. Four: every payer has its own contract, fee schedule, filing limits and authorisation rules, which is why RCM exists as a profession.",
   "**India vs US insurance, in short.** Indian health insurance is largely hospitalisation cover, often reimbursed to the patient or settled cashless through a third-party administrator at network hospitals; outpatient care is mostly paid in cash. US insurance covers outpatient and inpatient care alike, is billed by the provider, uses standard code sets (ICD-10-CM, CPT, HCPCS) and standard electronic transactions (837, 835, 270/271), and splits every allowed amount between payer and patient according to the policy."
  ],
  terms:[["Employer-sponsored insurance","Group coverage through a job; the largest source of coverage for Americans under 65."],["Uninsured","People with no coverage at all; they are billed as self-pay."],["Self-pay","A patient paying without insurance, often at a discounted self-pay rate."],["Third-party administrator (TPA)","In India, the company settling cashless claims; in the US, an administrator that processes claims for a self-funded employer plan."]],
  remember:"In the US the claim is a legal and financial document read by a computer first. Accuracy is not a courtesy, it is the product."},
 {id:"three-ps",title:"The 3Ps: Patient, Provider, Payer",
  line:"Every claim involves exactly three parties: the patient who receives care, the provider who delivers it, and the payer who pays for it.",
  body:[
   "**Patient**: any individual with a health complaint or illness who receives care. On a claim the patient may or may not be the same person as the insurance subscriber.",
   "**Provider**: any individual or organisation that provides medical services: general physicians, surgeons, nurse practitioners, hospitals, laboratories, imaging centres, therapists. Providers are identified on claims by their NPI (National Provider Identifier).",
   "**Payer (payor)**: the insurance company or government program that gives coverage for health risks and pays claims according to the policy. Medicare, Medicaid, Blue Cross, Aetna and UnitedHealthcare are all payers.",
   "RCM is the loop that connects the three: the patient enrols with the payer, the provider treats the patient, the provider bills the payer, and the payer pays the provider and tells the patient what is left to pay."
  ],
  terms:[["Patient","The person receiving care."],["Provider","The person or facility rendering care."],["Payer / Payor","The insurer or program responsible for paying the claim."],
   ["NPI","National Provider Identifier: a unique 10-digit number for every provider (Type 1 individual, Type 2 organisation)."],
   ["Tax ID (TIN/EIN)","The provider organisation's tax identification number used on claims for payment."]],
  remember:"When a claim denies, ask which P the problem belongs to: the patient's coverage, the provider's enrolment or documentation, or the payer's rules."},
 {id:"patient-terms",title:"Patient terms you will hear every day",
  line:"New vs established, inpatient vs outpatient, and the three signatures on every registration form: assignment of benefits, release of information and the financial agreement.",
  body:[
   "**New patient**: a patient who has not received any professional service from the physician, or another physician of the same specialty in the same group, within the past three years (36 months). **Established patient**: seen within the past three years. The distinction changes which E/M codes are billed, so it matters to payment.",
   "**Inpatient**: a patient formally admitted to a hospital under a physician's admission order, typically expecting to stay past midnight. **Outpatient**: treated and released without an inpatient admission, which includes office visits, same-day surgery, emergency room visits and observation stays. The old rule of thumb is \"more than 24 hours = inpatient\", but the admission order, not the clock, decides it.",
   "**Assignment of Benefits (AOB)**: the patient authorises the insurance company to pay the provider directly. Without it, the payer sends the cheque to the patient, which is exactly how the \"claim paid to patient\" denial scenario arises.",
   "**Release of Information (ROI)**: the patient authorises the provider to release medical information, but only for billing and payment purposes. This is what lets the billing team put a diagnosis on a claim.",
   "**Promissory note / financial agreement**: a signed written promise to pay a stated sum at a specified date or on demand. In registration paperwork it is the line saying the patient is responsible for charges the insurer does not pay."
  ],
  terms:[["New patient","Not seen by the physician or same-specialty group in 3 years."],["Established patient","Seen within the last 3 years."],
   ["Inpatient","Formally admitted to the hospital under an admission order."],["Outpatient","Treated without inpatient admission: office, ER, same-day surgery, observation."],
   ["Assignment of Benefits (AOB)","Patient directs the payer to pay the provider."],["Release of Information (ROI)","Patient permits release of medical information for billing."],
   ["Promissory note","Written promise to pay a stated amount."],["Guarantor","The person financially responsible for the bill, often the patient or a parent."]],
  remember:"No AOB on file means the money goes to the patient. Check the registration packet before chasing the payer."},
 {id:"providers-facilities",title:"Types of providers and facilities",
  line:"Claims name several providers in different roles, and the place where care happened changes how it is billed and paid.",
  body:[
   "**Primary care physician (PCP)**: the first point of contact for a patient and the \"gatekeeper\" between the patient and specialists. When the PCP sends a patient on, the PCP is the **referring provider**.",
   "**Attending provider**: the physician responsible for the patient's care during a hospital stay. **Ordering provider**: the physician (or qualified non-physician practitioner) who orders a service for the patient, such as X-rays, blood tests or urine tests. **Rendering provider**: the one who actually performed the billed service. **Specialist**: a doctor with advanced training in a specific area or organ system, such as a dentist, paediatrician or gynaecologist.",
   "**Specialties** are branches of medicine dealing with a body system or population: anesthesiology, cardiology, dermatology, gastroenterology, obstetrics and gynaecology, neurology, urology and nephrology, radiology, orthopaedics, chiropractic, endocrinology, paediatrics, and many more. Payers enrol providers by specialty (taxonomy code), which is why a specialty mismatch can deny a claim.",
   "**Facilities**: a **Skilled Nursing Facility (SNF)** meets federal criteria for Medicare and Medicaid payment for nursing care: physician supervision of every patient, at least one full-time registered nurse, full records, and nursing care 24 hours a day. An **Ambulatory Surgical Center (ASC)** performs surgery that does not need an overnight stay. **Home health** delivers skilled care in the patient's home, usually cheaper and just as effective as a facility. **Hospice** cares for terminally ill patients, focusing on comfort, pain and symptom relief, and emotional and spiritual needs rather than cure."
  ],
  terms:[["PCP / Referring provider","Gatekeeper who refers the patient onward."],["Attending provider","Physician in charge during an admission."],
   ["Ordering provider","Orders tests or services."],["Rendering provider","Performs the billed service."],["Specialist","Advanced training in one area of medicine."],
   ["Taxonomy code","10-character code describing a provider's specialty and type, used in payer enrolment."],
   ["Skilled Nursing Facility (SNF)","Federally certified nursing care with 24-hour nursing."],["Ambulatory Surgical Center (ASC)","Same-day surgery, no overnight stay."],
   ["Home health","Skilled care delivered at home."],["Hospice","End-of-life comfort care."]],
  remember:"On a CMS-1500 the referring/ordering provider goes in box 17 and the rendering provider in box 24J. Wrong NPI in either box is a common denial."},
 {id:"pos",title:"Place of service (POS) codes",
  line:"The two-digit place of service code tells the payer where the service happened. It must agree with the procedure code and the provider's setting.",
  body:[
   "Place of service is the location where services are rendered: inpatient, outpatient, emergency department, office, home and so on. Services can be rendered in a facility or outside one, and the payer pays differently for each because facility overhead is reimbursed separately.",
   "The code appears in box 24B of the CMS-1500. If the code contradicts the service (an inpatient-only procedure billed with POS 11, an office visit billed with POS 21), the claim rejects or denies for invalid place of service."
  ],
  compare:{head:["POS","Setting"],rows:[["02 / 10","Telehealth (02 other than home, 10 patient's home)"],["11","Office"],["12","Home"],["19","Off-campus outpatient hospital"],["20","Urgent care facility"],["21","Inpatient hospital"],["22","On-campus outpatient hospital"],["23","Emergency room, hospital"],["24","Ambulatory surgical center"],["25","Birthing center"],["31","Skilled nursing facility"],["32","Nursing facility"],["34","Hospice"],["81","Independent laboratory"]]},
  terms:[["Place of service (POS)","Two-digit code for where care was delivered, box 24B on the CMS-1500."],["Facility vs non-facility","Payer fee schedules pay less to the physician in a facility because the facility bills its own charges."]],
  remember:"POS 11 (office), 21 (inpatient), 22 (outpatient hospital) and 23 (ER) cover most claims. Learn those four first."}
]},
{id:"m2",short:"Insurance basics",title:"Insurance fundamentals",day:"Day 1",
 blurb:"The vocabulary of a health plan: who is covered, what they pay, which plan pays first.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"policy-people",title:"Policy, subscriber, dependent, beneficiary",
  line:"A policy is the contract between a person and a payer. The subscriber buys it, dependents are covered under it, and anyone eligible for benefits is a beneficiary.",
  body:[
   "**Insurance** is coverage given against a risk in current or future conditions. **Health insurance** is that coverage applied to health events. The **policy** is the written contract between the individual (or employer) and the payer that spells out what is covered and what each side pays.",
   "The **subscriber** (also called the insured, member or policyholder) is the person who buys or holds the policy. **Dependents** are the subscriber's spouse and children who are eligible for benefits under that policy. A **beneficiary** is any person entitled to benefits, which means either the subscriber or a dependent.",
   "The **premium** is what the subscriber (or employer) pays the insurer monthly, quarterly or yearly to keep the policy active. Premiums are not part of the claim; cost sharing at the point of care is."
  ],
  terms:[["Policy","Contract between the insured and the payer."],["Subscriber / Insured / Member","Holds the policy; their ID is the member ID."],["Dependent","Spouse or child covered under the subscriber's policy."],["Beneficiary","Anyone eligible for benefits under the policy."],["Premium","Periodic payment that keeps the policy in force."],["Member ID","The identifier printed on the insurance card that the payer uses to find the policy."],["Group number","Identifies the employer or group plan the policy belongs to."]],
  remember:"On the claim, the patient and the subscriber can be different people. Box 4 is the insured, box 2 is the patient, and box 6 states the relationship."},
 {id:"insurance-types",title:"Individual, group, federal, commercial and liability coverage",
  line:"Coverage is classified by who buys it (individual or group) and by who runs it (government, private company, or a liability insurer).",
  body:[
   "**Individual health insurance** is purchased by a person on an individual or family basis: one subscriber with one or more dependents, and the individual pays the premium. Medicare and Medicaid also behave as individual coverage: one person, one ID.",
   "**Group health insurance** is a plan providing coverage to employees, former employees and their families, sponsored by an employer or employee organisation. Features: multiple subscribers (the employees), premium paid wholly or mostly by the employer, each subscriber may have dependents, and employees and their families are the beneficiaries. Most private coverage in the US is employment-based.",
   "**Federal (government) insurance** is run and funded by government: Medicare, Medicaid, TRICARE, CHAMPVA, CHIP and others. The federal government is the largest single payer in the US, and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) decides what Medicare covers and how much it pays.",
   "**Commercial insurance** is sold by private companies such as Blue Cross Blue Shield, Aetna, Cigna, UnitedHealthcare and Humana, either individually or through a group plan.",
   "**Liability insurance** protects a person against claims arising from an accident: auto or no-fault insurance, homeowner's liability, and **workers' compensation** for injuries at work. When an injury is accident-related, the liability carrier pays before the health plan."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Type","Who pays the premium","Subscribers","Dependents"],rows:[["Individual","The individual","One","Allowed"],["Group / employer","Employer (mostly)","Many employees","Allowed"],["Workers' compensation","Employer","Each injured worker","None"],["Federal programs","Taxes (plus Part B premiums)","One per ID","Not under the same ID"]]},
  terms:[["Individual insurance","Bought by one person for self and family."],["Group insurance","Employer- or association-sponsored plan covering many subscribers."],["Federal insurance","Government-run programs such as Medicare and Medicaid."],["Commercial insurance","Private insurer products."],["Liability insurance","Covers losses from accidents; pays before health insurance for accident-related care."]],
  remember:"Workers' compensation has no dependents and the worker pays nothing. A WC claim with a copay on it is wrong."},
 {id:"key-dates",title:"Dates that decide whether a claim pays",
  line:"Coverage is checked on the date of service against the policy's effective and termination dates. Pre-existing condition waiting periods used to matter; the ACA removed them for most plans.",
  body:[
   "**Date of service (DOS)**: the date the patient received the service. Every eligibility question is asked about this date. **Enrollment date**: when the subscriber signed up with the insurer. **Effective date** (start date): when coverage begins to pay for healthcare expenses. **Termination date** (expiration date): when the policy ends. A DOS before the effective date or after the termination date denies as \"coverage not in effect\".",
   "A **pre-existing condition** is an illness the patient had before taking the policy, such as diabetes, cancer or asthma. A **waiting period** is the span of time a patient must wait before the insurer covers a pre-existing condition. Since 2014 the Affordable Care Act prohibits health plans from refusing coverage or imposing pre-existing condition exclusions, so these mostly survive in short-term, supplemental and some grandfathered plans.",
   "Other dates billers live by: the **timely filing limit** (how long after the DOS a claim may be submitted, from 90 days to a year depending on payer) and the **appeal deadline** printed on the denial."
  ],
  terms:[["Date of service (DOS)","Date the care was delivered."],["Enrollment date","Date the subscriber enrolled with the payer."],["Effective date","First date the policy pays."],["Termination date","Last date the policy pays."],["Pre-existing condition","Condition present before the policy began."],["Waiting period","Delay before certain benefits start."],["Timely filing limit (TFL)","Payer's deadline for receiving the initial claim."]],
  remember:"Always verify eligibility for the DOS, not for today. A patient can be active today and have been terminated on the day you treated them."},
 {id:"cost-sharing",title:"Cost sharing: copay, coinsurance, deductible, out-of-pocket maximum",
  line:"Cost sharing is the patient's part of the allowed amount. Copay is a flat fee per visit, deductible is paid first each year, coinsurance is a percentage after that, and the out-of-pocket maximum caps it all.",
  body:[
   "**Copay (copayment)**: a fixed dollar amount the patient pays at each visit, collected at the front desk. Different copays often apply for primary care, specialist, urgent care and ER visits.",
   "**Deductible**: a fixed annual amount the patient must pay before the insurer starts paying. A $1,000 deductible means the patient owes the first $1,000 of allowed charges in the plan year.",
   "**Coinsurance**: a percentage split of the allowed amount between payer and patient after the deductible is met, written payer:patient, for example 80:20.",
   "**Out-of-pocket maximum (OOP max)**: the most the patient pays in a plan year for covered in-network services. Once reached, the plan pays 100% of allowed amounts. Deductible, coinsurance and copays count toward it; premiums do not.",
   "All of this applies to the **allowed amount**, the maximum the payer recognises for the service under its fee schedule or contract, never to the billed charge. A worked example: billed $500, allowed $300, deductible met, 80:20 plan. Payer pays $240, patient owes $60, and the $200 difference is a contractual adjustment for an in-network provider."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Term","What it is","When it applies"],rows:[["Copay","Fixed amount per visit","At every visit, usually before the deductible"],["Deductible","Fixed annual amount before the plan pays","Start of each plan year until met"],["Coinsurance","Percentage of the allowed amount","After the deductible, until the OOP max"],["OOP maximum","Annual cap on patient cost sharing","Once reached, plan pays 100%"]]},
  terms:[["Copay","Flat per-visit fee."],["Deductible","Annual amount the patient pays first."],["Coinsurance","Percentage share after the deductible, e.g. 80:20."],["Out-of-pocket maximum","Yearly cap on the patient's cost sharing."],["Allowed amount","Maximum the payer recognises for a service."],["Patient responsibility","Copay + deductible + coinsurance owed by the patient, shown as PR group codes on the remittance."]],
  remember:"Coinsurance is a share of the allowed amount, never the billed amount. 20% of a $150 allowed is $30 even if the charge was $400."},
 {id:"financial-responsibility",title:"Determining financial responsibility: billed, allowed, benefit maximums and visit limits",
  line:"Who owes what on a claim is decided in a fixed order: the allowed amount is set, benefit limits are checked, then the allowed amount is split between the payer and the patient.",
  body:[
   "**Billed amount (charge)**: the provider's full fee for the service, from its chargemaster or fee schedule. It is the starting number on every claim and is almost never what gets paid.",
   "**Allowed amount**: the maximum the payer recognises for that service under its contract or fee schedule. For in-network providers everything above it is a contractual adjustment. Medicare's allowed amounts come from the Physician Fee Schedule; commercial contracts are often expressed as a percentage of Medicare.",
   "**Benefit maximum**: a cap on what the plan pays for a benefit, per year or per lifetime, such as $1,500 of dental care a year or $2,000 of hearing aids every three years. Once the maximum is exhausted, further charges are patient responsibility. The ACA bans dollar maximums on essential health benefits, so these survive mainly in dental, vision and some therapy benefits.",
   "**Visit limits**: a cap on the number of covered visits, such as 20 physical therapy visits or 30 chiropractic visits per year. Visits beyond the limit deny as \"benefit maximum reached\" and fall to the patient, unless an authorised extension exists.",
   "The order of operations when a payer adjudicates: confirm eligibility on the DOS; confirm the service is covered and authorised; set the allowed amount; apply any benefit maximum or visit limit; apply the deductible, then coinsurance or copay; pay the rest. The patient's responsibility is what the deductible, coinsurance, copay and non-covered amounts add up to.",
   "Worked example: billed $900, allowed $600, deductible remaining $200, 80:20 coinsurance, in network. Deductible takes $200 from the allowed; the remaining $400 splits $320 payer and $80 patient. Payer pays $320, patient owes $280, and $300 is contractual adjustment."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Line on the EOB","Example","Who it belongs to"],rows:[["Billed amount","$900","Starting point only"],["Allowed amount","$600","Basis for everything below"],["Contractual adjustment (CO-45)","$300","Provider writes off"],["Deductible (PR-1)","$200","Patient"],["Coinsurance (PR-2)","$80","Patient"],["Paid by payer","$320","Provider receives"]]},
  terms:[["Billed amount","Provider's full charge."],["Allowed amount","Payer's maximum for the service."],["Benefit maximum","Dollar cap on a benefit per year or lifetime."],["Visit limit","Cap on the number of covered visits for a service type."],["Fee schedule","A payer's list of allowed amounts by procedure code."],["Chargemaster","A hospital's master list of billed charges."]],
  remember:"Verify benefit maximums and visit limits during eligibility for therapy, chiropractic, dental and vision. Finding out on the EOB is too late."},
 {id:"par-nonpar",title:"In-network and out-of-network: contractual adjustment, write-off, balance billing",
  line:"A participating provider has a contract with the payer and accepts the allowed amount as payment in full. A non-participating provider has no contract and may bill the patient for the difference.",
  body:[
   "**Participating (par, in-network) providers** have entered an agreement with the insurance carrier: the carrier directs members to them and the provider accepts a lower, contracted fee. **Non-participating (non-par, out-of-network) providers** have declined a contract, often because the offered fee is lower than they will accept.",
   "**Contractual adjustment**: for a par provider, the difference between the billed amount and the allowed amount. It is adjusted off and never billed to the patient. On the remittance it appears with group code CO (contractual obligation), most often CO-45.",
   "**Write-off**: an amount the provider removes from the account without collecting it. For non-par providers this is the difference between billed and allowed that the practice chooses not to pursue. Write-offs also cover small balances, bad debt and timely filing losses.",
   "**Balance billing**: billing the patient for the difference between the billed charge and the allowed amount. Only non-par providers can do this, and only where the law allows. Medicare limits it, Medicaid forbids it, and the federal No Surprises Act (2022) bans it for emergency care and for out-of-network providers at in-network facilities."
  ],
  compare:{head:["","Par (in-network)","Non-par (out-of-network)"],rows:[["Contract with payer","Yes","No"],["Billed minus allowed","Contractual adjustment (CO-45)","Write-off, or balance bill the patient"],["Patient pays","Only copay/deductible/coinsurance","Possibly the full difference"],["Payment goes to","Provider","Provider if AOB, otherwise patient"]]},
  terms:[["Participating (par) provider","Contracted, in-network."],["Non-participating (non-par) provider","Not contracted, out-of-network."],["Contractual adjustment","Billed minus allowed for a par provider; not billable to the patient."],["Write-off","Amount removed from the account without collection."],["Balance billing","Billing the patient for billed minus allowed (non-par only)."],["No Surprises Act","2022 federal law banning most surprise out-of-network balance bills."]],
  remember:"CO means the provider absorbs it, PR means the patient owes it. Never move a CO amount to the patient."},
 {id:"contracts-networks",title:"Provider contracts, networks and credentialing",
  line:"A provider gets paid in-network rates only after being credentialed and enrolled with the payer and signing a contract that sets the fee schedule and the rules.",
  body:[
   "**Credentialing** is the payer's verification of a provider's licence, education, training, work history, malpractice coverage and sanctions, usually through CAQH ProView. **Enrollment** (sometimes called contracting or participation) is the payer loading the provider into its system as a participating provider with an effective date. Together they take 60 to 180 days.",
   "The **contract** (participating provider agreement) sets the **fee schedule** (allowed amounts, often a percentage of Medicare), the **timely filing limit**, authorisation requirements, claim submission rules, how the provider can bill patients, and the termination terms. **Network** is the set of contracted providers a plan's members can use at in-network benefit levels; a plan may run several networks (HMO, PPO, narrow).",
   "Claims filed before the enrollment effective date, under the wrong group NPI or TIN, or for a provider whose contract has lapsed, deny as \"provider not certified or eligible\" or pay out of network. **Re-credentialing** repeats every two to three years, and a missed deadline silently ends participation.",
   "Medicare enrollment runs through PECOS; a provider may **accept assignment** (take the Medicare allowed amount as payment in full) or not. **Opting out** of Medicare means no Medicare claims at all."
  ],
  terms:[["Credentialing","Payer verification of a provider's qualifications."],["Enrollment","Loading the provider into the payer's system with an effective date."],["Participating provider agreement","The contract between provider and payer."],["Fee schedule","Contracted allowed amounts by code."],["Network","The set of contracted providers for a plan."],["CAQH","Council for Affordable Quality Healthcare: the shared credentialing database most payers use."],["PECOS","Medicare's provider enrollment system."],["Accepting assignment","Agreeing to accept the Medicare allowed amount as payment in full."]],
  remember:"Before appealing a \"provider not eligible\" denial, check the enrollment effective date against the DOS. The DOS is usually the problem."},
 {id:"ancillary-plans",title:"Ancillary plans: dental, vision, pharmacy and more",
  line:"Dental, vision, hearing, pharmacy and behavioural health are often carved out to separate plans or administrators, each with its own ID card, claim form and rules.",
  body:[
   "An **ancillary plan** covers a benefit outside standard medical coverage. **Dental** plans use the ADA claim form and CDT codes, typically with an annual maximum around $1,000 to $2,000. **Vision** plans (VSP, EyeMed, Davis Vision) cover exams and eyewear on a yearly or two-yearly schedule. **Hearing** benefits often work as a dollar allowance. **Pharmacy** benefits are run by a **pharmacy benefit manager (PBM)** such as CVS Caremark, Express Scripts or Optum Rx; the RxBIN, RxPCN and RxGroup on the card route the pharmacy claim.",
   "**Behavioural health** (mental health and substance use) is frequently **carved out** to a managed behavioural health organisation (Carelon, Magellan, Optum Behavioral Health) even when the medical plan is with another payer. Billing the medical payer for a carved-out service produces a denial telling you to bill the other administrator.",
   "**Supplemental** policies (accident, critical illness, hospital indemnity, Medigap) pay the patient or cover cost sharing; they are secondary and need the primary EOB."
  ],
  terms:[["Ancillary plan","Separate coverage for dental, vision, hearing or pharmacy."],["Carve-out","A benefit administered by a different company than the medical plan."],["Pharmacy benefit manager (PBM)","Administrator of the drug benefit; identified by RxBIN/RxPCN on the card."],["CDT codes","Current Dental Terminology codes used on dental claims."],["Supplemental policy","Extra coverage that pays cost sharing or a fixed cash benefit."]],
  remember:"Read the back of the insurance card. Carve-outs and PBMs are listed there with their own claims addresses and payer IDs."},
 {id:"managed-care",title:"Managed care plans: HMO, PPO, POS, EPO",
  line:"Managed care plans contract with networks of providers to control cost. They differ in whether a PCP is required, whether referrals are needed, and whether out-of-network care is covered.",
  body:[
   "**Managed care plans** are health plans that hold contracts with providers and facilities to deliver care to members at reduced cost. The trade-off for the member is rules about which providers they can see.",
   "**HMO (Health Maintenance Organization)**: the member must choose a PCP, needs a referral from the PCP to see a specialist, and has no out-of-network benefits except emergencies. Lowest premiums and out-of-pocket costs. Exception: women do not need a referral for in-network OB/GYN routine care such as Pap tests, well-woman visits and obstetric care.",
   "**PPO (Preferred Provider Organization)**: no PCP, no referrals, and out-of-network care is covered at a lower benefit level. In-network means smaller out-of-pocket costs; out-of-network means bigger ones. Highest premiums.",
   "**POS (Point of Service)**: a hybrid. A PCP and referrals are required like an HMO, but out-of-network care is covered like a PPO, at higher cost to the member.",
   "**EPO (Exclusive Provider Organization)**: no PCP or referrals like a PPO, but no out-of-network coverage like an HMO.",
   "**Referral vs prior authorization**: a referral is the PCP's permission to see a specialist. A prior authorization (pre-certification) is the payer's approval, obtained before the service, for a specific procedure, admission or drug. Any plan type can require prior authorization for expensive services; HMO and POS plans add the referral requirement on top."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Criteria","HMO","PPO","POS","EPO"],rows:[["PCP required","Yes","No","Yes","No"],["Referral to see a specialist","Yes","No","Yes","No"],["Out-of-network coverage","Emergencies only","Yes, at higher cost","Yes, at higher cost","Emergencies only"],["Relative cost to member","Lowest","Highest","Middle","Low"]]},
  terms:[["Managed care","Insurance built on contracted provider networks."],["HMO","PCP, referrals, in-network only."],["PPO","No PCP, no referrals, out-of-network covered."],["POS","PCP and referrals, out-of-network covered."],["EPO","No PCP or referrals, in-network only."],["Referral","PCP's authorisation to see a specialist."],["Prior authorization","Payer's advance approval for a service."],["Out-of-network (OON)","Provider without a contract with the member's plan."]],
  remember:"Two of the most common denials, \"no authorization\" and \"referral absent\", come straight from these plan rules. Check the plan type during eligibility verification."},
 {id:"cob",title:"Coordination of benefits: which plan pays first",
  line:"When a patient has more than one plan, coordination of benefits decides the order. The primary pays first up to its limits, the secondary pays what is left, and a tertiary pays after that.",
  body:[
   "**Coordination of benefits (COB)** is the set of rules deciding the order in which plans pay when a person is covered by more than one policy. The **primary insurance** pays first and pays the most; the **secondary** pays the remaining amount after the primary's payment, subject to its own rules; a **tertiary** pays after the secondary. The patient must tell each insurer about the others, and billers must bill them in order, attaching the primary EOB to the secondary claim.",
   "The main rules: a plan that covers the patient as the **subscriber (employee)** is primary over a plan that covers them as a **dependent**. For a dependent child covered by both parents, the **birthday rule** applies: the parent whose birthday (month and day) comes first in the calendar year is primary; if both share a birthday, the plan that has covered a parent longer is primary. Divorced parents follow the court decree. **Medicaid is always the payer of last resort**. For accident-related care, **no-fault, auto or liability insurance** and **workers' compensation** pay before any health plan.",
   "**Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP)**: Medicare is secondary when the patient is 65 or older and covered by an employer group health plan through their own or a spouse's current employment at an employer with 20 or more employees; when the patient is disabled and covered by a large group plan (100+ employees); during the first 30 months of ESRD-based entitlement when a group plan exists; and whenever workers' compensation, no-fault or liability insurance covers the injury. Retiree coverage and employers under 20 employees leave Medicare primary.",
   "**Supplementary insurance** (Medigap and similar) is sold by private companies to pay costs the primary plan leaves to the patient, such as copays, coinsurance and deductibles. It is always secondary."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Patient has","Scenario","Primary","Secondary"],rows:[
   ["Medicare and Medicaid","Any covered service","Medicare","Medicaid"],
   ["Medicare and an employer plan (20+ employees, actively working)","Not work related","Employer group plan","Medicare"],
   ["Medicare and an employer plan (under 20 employees, or retiree coverage)","Not work related","Medicare","Employer plan"],
   ["Any health plan","Work-related injury","Workers' compensation","Health plan only for non-covered items"],
   ["Medicare or a commercial plan, plus no-fault or liability insurance","Accident","No-fault / liability insurer","Health plan"],
   ["Medicare and TRICARE For Life","Covered by both","Medicare","TRICARE For Life"],
   ["Two commercial plans","Subscriber on one, dependent on the other","Plan where patient is the subscriber","Plan where patient is a dependent"],
   ["Child on both parents' plans","Different birthdays","Parent with the earlier birthday in the year","Other parent's plan"],
   ["Child on both parents' plans","Same birthday","Plan that has covered a parent longer","The newer plan"]]},
  terms:[["Coordination of benefits (COB)","Rules for the order plans pay when there is more than one."],["Primary insurance","Pays first, up to its limits."],["Secondary insurance","Pays the balance left by the primary, within its own rules."],["Tertiary insurance","Pays after the secondary."],["Birthday rule","For a child on both parents' plans, the parent born earlier in the year is primary."],["Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP)","Situations where another payer must pay before Medicare."],["Payer of last resort","Medicaid: pays only after every other coverage."],["Crossover","Primary payer automatically forwards the claim to the secondary."]],
  remember:"Secondary denials for \"primary EOB required\" are COB failures. Confirm the order during eligibility verification and bill in sequence."}
]},
{id:"mc",short:"Compliance",title:"HIPAA, PHI and the Affordable Care Act",day:"Day 1",
 blurb:"The two laws every biller must know: one protects the patient's information, the other reshaped who has coverage.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"hipaa",title:"HIPAA in ten points",
  line:"HIPAA, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996, sets national rules for the privacy and security of patients' health information. Billing teams handle that information all day, so HIPAA applies to you directly.",
  body:[
   "HIPAA was passed in 1996 to establish national standards for handling medical records and other **protected health information (PHI)**. It applies to **covered entities**: healthcare providers (doctors, hospitals), **health plans** (payers) and **healthcare clearinghouses** that transmit health information electronically, and, through **business associate agreements**, to vendors such as billing companies. Purview is a business associate of every client.",
   "The **Privacy Rule** says how PHI may be used and disclosed. Using or sharing PHI for **treatment, payment and healthcare operations (TPO)** is permitted without special consent; anything else needs the patient's written authorisation. Only the **minimum necessary** information may be used for a task: a payer call needs the member ID, DOS and CPT codes, not the whole chart. Patients have the right to access and obtain copies of their records, and to ask for corrections.",
   "The **Security Rule** requires administrative, physical and technical safeguards for electronic PHI: locked screens, unique logins, encryption, no PHI in personal email or chat, no photographs of screens. The **Breach Notification Rule** requires affected patients, HHS and sometimes the media to be told when PHI is exposed.",
   "**Penalties** range from fines per violation (tiered by negligence, up to seven figures per year) to criminal charges for knowingly obtaining or disclosing PHI. Violations happen through unauthorised access to records, snooping on a relative's or celebrity's chart, lost unencrypted laptops, and misdirected faxes or emails.",
   "The goal is balance: keep patients' information confidential while still allowing the sharing needed for treatment, payment and operations."
  ],
  terms:[["HIPAA","Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, 1996."],["Protected health information (PHI)","Individually identifiable health information held or transmitted by a covered entity, in any form."],["Covered entity","Providers, health plans and clearinghouses bound by HIPAA."],["Business associate","A vendor that handles PHI for a covered entity under a signed agreement (a billing company, for example)."],["TPO","Treatment, payment and healthcare operations: the uses of PHI that need no separate authorisation."],["Minimum necessary","Use or disclose only the PHI needed for the task."],["Privacy Rule / Security Rule","HIPAA's rules for how PHI is used, and how electronic PHI is protected."],["Breach","An impermissible use or disclosure of PHI that compromises its privacy or security."]],
  tips:["Verify you are speaking to the payer or the patient (two identifiers: name plus DOB or member ID) before discussing a claim.","Never leave claim details in a voicemail beyond a call-back request.","Report a suspected breach the same day. Delay is treated as a second violation."],
  remember:"If the information is not needed to get the claim paid, you do not need to see it, say it or send it."},
 {id:"phi",title:"What counts as PHI: the 18 identifiers",
  line:"Health information becomes protected when it can be tied to a person. HIPAA lists 18 identifiers; if any one of them travels with health data, the whole record is PHI.",
  body:[
   "The 18 HIPAA identifiers: names; geographic data smaller than a state (street address, city, county, ZIP code); all dates directly related to a person (birth, admission, discharge, death) and any age over 89; telephone numbers; fax numbers; email addresses; Social Security numbers; medical record numbers; health plan beneficiary (member) numbers; account numbers; certificate or licence numbers; vehicle identifiers and plates; device identifiers and serial numbers; web URLs; IP addresses; biometric identifiers such as fingerprints and voice prints; full-face photographs; and any other unique identifying number, characteristic or code.",
   "A claim is PHI from end to end: it carries the name, DOB, member ID, address, diagnosis and procedure codes. An EOB is PHI. A call note with a member ID is PHI. A spreadsheet of denials with patient names is PHI, which is why it may never leave approved systems.",
   "**De-identified** data, with all 18 identifiers removed, is no longer PHI and may be used for reporting and training. This app's denial scenarios are de-identified for that reason."
  ],
  terms:[["18 identifiers","HIPAA's list of data elements that make health information identifiable."],["De-identification","Removing all 18 identifiers so data is no longer PHI."],["ePHI","PHI stored or transmitted electronically, covered by the Security Rule."]],
  remember:"Member ID plus a CPT code is PHI. Treat screenshots, notes and spreadsheets accordingly."},
 {id:"aca",title:"The Affordable Care Act in eight points",
  line:"The Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (ACA, \"Obamacare\") was signed on 23 March 2010 to widen coverage, improve quality and slow cost growth. It changed who your patients are and what their plans must cover.",
  body:[
   "**1. Coverage expansion.** The ACA created the Health Insurance **Marketplace** (exchange) where individuals buy plans, with income-based premium subsidies, and let states expand **Medicaid** to adults up to 138% of the federal poverty level. The uninsured rate roughly halved.",
   "**2. Guaranteed issue and no pre-existing condition exclusions.** Insurers must sell to everyone and cannot deny, limit or charge more because of health status or gender.",
   "**3. Essential health benefits.** Marketplace and small-group plans must cover ten categories, including hospitalisation, emergency care, maternity, mental health, prescription drugs, lab services and paediatric care.",
   "**4. Preventive care at no cost.** Screenings, immunisations, well-woman visits and family planning are covered with no copay or deductible when delivered in network. Billing them with the right preventive codes and modifier 33 matters, or the patient gets a bill they should not.",
   "**5. Dependents to age 26** can stay on a parent's plan. **6. No annual or lifetime dollar limits** on essential benefits. **7. Metal tiers** (Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum) describe how much of the cost the plan pays on average, from about 60% to 90%. **8. Medical loss ratio**: insurers must spend at least 80 to 85% of premiums on care, or refund the difference.",
   "For billers the ACA shows up as more insured patients, high-deductible Bronze plans with large patient balances, Marketplace plans with a 90-day **grace period** for subsidised members (claims in months two and three may pend until the premium is paid), and strict preventive-care coding."
  ],
  terms:[["ACA / PPACA / Obamacare","Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act, 2010."],["Marketplace / exchange","Where individuals buy ACA plans, federally (HealthCare.gov) or through a state exchange."],["Essential health benefits","Ten service categories every ACA-compliant plan must cover."],["Metal tiers","Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum: plan generosity levels."],["Grace period","90 days for subsidised Marketplace members to pay overdue premiums; claims may pend in months two and three."],["Medical loss ratio (MLR)","Minimum share of premiums an insurer must spend on care."]],
  remember:"A Marketplace patient whose eligibility shows \"pending premium\" is in the grace period. Verify again before the claim is written off."}
]},
{id:"m3",short:"Revenue cycle",title:"The revenue cycle at a glance",day:"Day 2",
 blurb:"Thirteen steps from the appointment to the last dollar collected, and who owns each one.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"rcm-overview",title:"What revenue cycle management is",
  line:"Revenue cycle management (RCM) is every administrative and clinical step that captures, bills and collects the money owed for patient care, from the first phone call to a zero balance.",
  body:[
   "The cycle has three phases. **Front end** (provider office): appointment scheduling and pre-registration, registration, eligibility and benefits verification, and the encounter itself. **Mid cycle**: medical documentation, medical coding, demographic and charge entry. **Back end** (mostly the billing organisation): claim submission, clearinghouse scrubbing and rejections, payer adjudication, payment posting, accounts receivable follow-up, denial management, credit balances and patient collections.",
   "Each step hands a document to the next: the appointment creates the demographic record, registration adds the facesheet and signatures, verification adds the benefits, the encounter produces the medical record, coding turns it into codes on a superbill, charge entry turns the superbill into a claim, the clearinghouse turns the claim into an 837 file, the payer answers with an 835 remittance, posting turns that into balances, and AR works whatever is left.",
   "Why the order matters: most denials are born at the front end. A wrong member ID at registration, an unverified authorisation, or a missed secondary plan surfaces weeks later as a denial that costs far more to fix than it would have cost to prevent. Good RCM pushes work to the earliest step where the problem can be caught."
  ],
  compare:{head:["#","Step","Phase","Usually done by","Output"],rows:[
   ["1","Appointment scheduling and pre-registration","Front end","Provider office","Demographics, insurance details, appointment"],
   ["2","Eligibility and benefits verification","Front end","Office or MBO","Verified coverage, copay, deductible, auth needs"],
   ["3","Encounter","Front end","Provider","Medical record, diagnosis, treatment plan"],
   ["4","Medical coding","Mid cycle","Coders","ICD-10-CM, CPT/HCPCS, modifiers"],
   ["5","Demographic and charge entry","Mid cycle","MBO","Charges on the patient account"],
   ["6","Claim submission","Back end","MBO","CMS-1500 / UB-04 as 837 file"],
   ["7","Clearinghouse scrubbing and rejections","Back end","Clearinghouse + MBO","Clean claims forwarded, rejections fixed"],
   ["8","Insurance adjudication","Back end","Payer","Paid, denied, partially paid, pended"],
   ["9","Payment posting","Back end","MBO","EOB/ERA amounts applied, balances set"],
   ["10","Accounts receivable follow-up","Back end","MBO","Status on every unpaid claim"],
   ["11","Denial management","Back end","MBO","Corrected claims, appeals, root-cause fixes"],
   ["12","Credit balance","Back end","MBO","Refunds, corrected postings"],
   ["13","Patient collections","Back end","MBO / agency","Statements, payments, agency referrals"]]},
  terms:[["Revenue cycle management (RCM)","The end-to-end process of getting paid for care."],["Front end / mid cycle / back end","The three phases of the cycle."],["Clean claim","A claim that passes every edit and pays on first submission."],["First-pass resolution rate","Share of claims paid without rework; the headline measure of a healthy cycle."],["Days in AR","Average days a charge waits to be paid: total AR divided by average daily charges."],["Net collection rate","Payments collected as a share of what the payer contracts allowed; measures leakage."]],
  remember:"Fix problems at the step that created them. A denial worked in AR is a registration or coding failure that escaped."}
]},
{id:"m4",short:"Front end",title:"Front end: before and during the visit",day:"Day 2",
 blurb:"Scheduling, registration, eligibility verification and the encounter: where the claim is either set up to pay or set up to fail.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"scheduling",step:1,title:"Appointment scheduling and pre-registration",
  line:"The patient books by phone, online or as a walk-in. Pre-registration collects demographics and insurance before the visit so eligibility can be checked and the patient reminded.",
  body:[
   "Appointment scheduling is the patient's first contact with the practice: by phone, through a portal, or in person at the front desk. **Pre-registration** uses that contact to collect the data the claim will need: legal name, date of birth, sex, address, phone, guarantor, insurance company, member ID, group number, subscriber name and relationship, and the reason for the visit.",
   "Capturing a photo or scan of the insurance card front and back, and a photo ID, at this stage prevents most identity and member-ID denials later. The office also notes whether the visit needs a referral or prior authorisation, and schedules a reminder call one day before the appointment to cut no-shows."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Eligibility and benefits verification",steps:["Patient makes an appointment with the provider (phone, online or walk-in)","Front desk collects demographics and insurance information and schedules the visit with pre-registration","Information is gathered for eligibility and benefits verification with the insurer","A reminder call is scheduled for the day before the appointment"]},
  terms:[["Pre-registration","Collecting patient and insurance data before the visit."],["Demographics","Name, DOB, sex, address, phone and similar identifying data."],["Guarantor","Person responsible for the bill."],["No-show","A missed appointment; many practices bill a no-show fee to the patient, never to the payer."]],
  remember:"Every field on the facesheet maps to a box on the claim. Ask for the spelling on the insurance card, not how the patient usually writes it."},
 {id:"registration",title:"Registration and the facesheet",
  line:"At check-in the patient completes or confirms the registration form, also called the facesheet. It carries the demographics, insurance details and the signatures that let the provider bill.",
  body:[
   "The front desk first asks: **new or established patient?** A new patient completes the full registration form; an established patient confirms that nothing has changed (address, employer, insurance). Then the patient waits to meet the provider.",
   "A **facesheet** contains: **patient information** (name, DOB, SSN, phone, sex, marital status, address, employer); **guarantor or responsible party** information with relationship to the patient; **emergency contact**; **provider information** (primary care or referring physician, reason for visit); **insurance information** for primary and secondary plans (payer, subscriber, member ID, group), with the card attached; and the **assignment and release** statement.",
   "That statement combines the AOB and ROI: the patient assigns medical benefits to the practice, authorises release of information needed to secure payment (including sensitive diagnoses), acknowledges that outside labs or radiologists may bill separately, and agrees to be financially responsible for charges not paid by insurance. The signature and date make it valid."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Encounter",steps:["Front desk asks whether the patient is new or established","Patient completes (new) or confirms (established) the registration form","Insurance card and photo ID are copied; AOB and ROI are signed","Patient is checked in and waits for the provider"]},
  terms:[["Facesheet","The registration form summarising demographics, insurance and signatures."],["Responsible party","Guarantor for the account."],["Assignment and release","Combined AOB and ROI statement signed by the patient."],["Check-in","Confirming arrival, collecting the copay and verifying details on the day."]],
  remember:"Collect the copay at check-in. Money not collected at the desk costs three statements and a phone call to collect later."},
 {id:"eligibility",step:2,title:"Eligibility check and benefits verification",
  line:"Before the visit, confirm with the payer that the patient is covered on the date of service and find out exactly what the plan pays for the planned service. Done well, this prevents the biggest denial categories.",
  body:[
   "**Eligibility** answers whether the policy is active on the DOS and who the subscriber is. **Benefits verification** answers what the plan pays for this service: copay, deductible remaining, coinsurance, out-of-pocket met, whether the provider is in network, whether a referral or prior authorisation is required, visit limits, and whether another plan is primary (COB).",
   "Verification is done through the payer portal, an electronic 270/271 eligibility transaction from the practice system or clearinghouse, or a phone call to the payer. The practice management screen typically shows each policy with payer, plan, member and subscriber IDs, effective and termination dates, copay and annual deductible.",
   "What to document: payer name and phone, policy and group numbers, effective date, plan type (HMO, PPO, POS), PCP on file, copay by visit type, deductible total and remaining, coinsurance, out-of-pocket remaining, network status, authorisation or referral requirement with the number if one exists, the rep's name and the call reference number. The patient is then told their expected responsibility and acknowledges it."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Encounter",steps:["Review the insurance information collected at scheduling","Cross-check with the payer (portal, 270/271 or phone) and verify eligibility and plan benefits for the service to be rendered","Document the verified eligibility and benefits and obtain the patient's acknowledgment of their responsibility","Provider's office enters the information to be sent to the billing organisation"]},
  terms:[["Eligibility","Whether coverage is active on the date of service."],["Benefits verification","What the plan pays for the planned service and what the patient owes."],["270 / 271","The HIPAA electronic eligibility inquiry and response transactions."],["Prior authorization number","Payer's approval reference; it must appear on the claim (box 23)."],["Referral number","PCP's referral reference for HMO/POS plans."],["Network status","Whether the rendering provider is in or out of the patient's plan network."]],
  tips:["Verify for the exact DOS and the exact rendering provider and location.","Ask \"is there any other coverage on file?\" on every verification; it catches COB problems early.","Re-verify at the start of a new month for Medicaid and for any patient whose plan year resets."],
  remember:"Eligibility active plus benefits verified plus authorisation on file equals a claim that is hard to deny."},
 {id:"encounter",step:3,title:"The encounter and the medical record",
  line:"The encounter is the clinical contact between patient and provider. Its documentation is the medical record, and nothing can be billed that is not documented there.",
  body:[
   "An **encounter** is a healthcare contact between the patient and the provider responsible for diagnosing and treating the patient. The provider takes a history, examines the patient, forms a diagnosis, designs a treatment plan and renders treatment. Everything billed must trace back to this documentation: \"if it is not documented, it was not done.\"",
   "**How records are created.** The older method is **medical transcription**: the physician dictates voice files, a transcriptionist converts them to text, and the text becomes the record. The current method is the **electronic health record (EHR)** or **electronic medical record (EMR)**: a digital chart containing the patient's history, notes, orders, results and medications. Strictly, an EMR is one practice's digital chart, while an EHR is designed to share across organisations; in daily use the words are interchangeable.",
   "For billers the record matters in three ways: it supports the codes chosen (medical necessity), it is what the payer asks for in a records request or audit, and the signed, dated note with the rendering provider's name is what makes the claim defensible."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Medical coding",steps:["Patient completes registration and waits for the provider","Provider meets the patient: history, discussion and examination","Diagnosis is concluded and a treatment plan designed","Treatment is rendered, the note is signed, and the registration documents and record are sent to the billing organisation"]},
  terms:[["Encounter","A documented clinical contact between patient and provider."],["Medical record / chart","The complete documentation of the patient's care."],["Medical transcription","Converting dictated voice files into text records."],["EHR / EMR","Electronic health or medical record."],["Medical necessity","The clinical justification, in the record, for a billed service."],["Provider signature","Authentication of the note; unsigned notes do not support a claim."]],
  remember:"Coders code from the note, not from the superbill alone. A service circled on a superbill with no note behind it cannot be billed."}
]},
{id:"m5",short:"Coding & charge entry",title:"Medical coding and charge entry",day:"Day 2",
 blurb:"Turning the medical record into standard codes, then into charges on the patient's account.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"coding-overview",step:4,title:"Medical coding: diagnoses, procedures and modifiers",
  line:"Medical coding translates the record into standard codes: ICD-10-CM for the diagnosis (why), CPT and HCPCS for the procedure or service (what), and modifiers for the circumstances (how).",
  body:[
   "Coders read the encounter note and assign: a **diagnosis code (Dx)** from ICD-10-CM, such as S35.2 or M54.50, telling the payer why the patient was treated; a **procedure code (Px)** from CPT or HCPCS, such as 99214, 54264 or A6985, telling the payer what was done; and **modifiers**, two characters appended to the procedure code to describe special circumstances.",
   "The diagnosis must support the procedure: the payer checks that the Dx makes the Px medically necessary. A chest X-ray with a diagnosis of ankle sprain denies as \"diagnosis inconsistent with procedure\". **Linkage** on the claim (the diagnosis pointer in box 24E) tells the payer which diagnosis justifies each line.",
   "Coding is done by certified coders (CPC, CCS) or by the physician through the EHR, and is checked by coding audits. The output, the coded encounter with patient and visit details, is the **superbill** or charge ticket that goes to charge entry."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Demographic and charge entry",steps:["The medical record is uploaded or made available to the coding team","Coders convert the record into alphanumeric codes: ICD-10-CM, CPT/HCPCS and modifiers","Coded files with the registration documents are sent for charge entry as a superbill"]},
  terms:[["Diagnosis code (Dx)","ICD-10-CM code for the condition treated."],["Procedure code (Px)","CPT or HCPCS code for the service performed."],["Modifier","Two-character addition that changes or clarifies a procedure code."],["Medical necessity","The Dx must justify the Px."],["Diagnosis pointer","Box 24E link from each service line to its supporting diagnosis."],["Superbill / charge ticket","The coded encounter summary used for charge entry."],["Upcoding / downcoding","Billing a higher or lower level than documented; both are compliance problems."]],
  remember:"ICD says why, CPT says what, modifiers say how. Denials for \"inconsistent with diagnosis\" are a linkage problem before they are a coding problem."},
 {id:"icd10",title:"ICD-10-CM: how a diagnosis code is built",
  line:"ICD-10-CM codes are three to seven characters: a letter, two digits, a decimal point, then up to four more characters adding cause, location, laterality and encounter type.",
  body:[
   "The International Classification of Diseases, 10th revision, Clinical Modification (**ICD-10-CM**) is the US diagnosis code set, maintained by the National Center for Health Statistics and updated every 1 October. There are over 70,000 codes.",
   "Structure: the **first character is a letter**; the **second and third are digits**; together they form the **category**. A **decimal point** follows the third character. Characters **four to seven** may be letters or digits and add detail: etiology, anatomic site, severity, laterality (right, left, bilateral) and, for injuries, the **seventh character extension**: A initial encounter, D subsequent encounter, S sequela. The placeholder **X** fills unused positions when a seventh character is required.",
   "Worked example **S42.321A**: S42 is the category (fracture of shoulder and upper arm); .3 is the shaft of the humerus; 2 is a displaced transverse fracture; 1 is the right arm; A is initial encounter for a closed fracture. Read it and you know the injury, where, which side and that this is the first visit.",
   "Letters: every letter is used. U was reserved for new diseases and emergency use and was activated in 2020 for COVID-19 (U07.1) and post-COVID conditions (U09.9). Chapters run from A00 to Z99; Z codes describe factors influencing health status, such as Z00.00 for a routine adult exam.",
   "Rules that produce denials when broken: code to the **highest specificity** available (an unspecified code where a specific one exists may deny); never code a \"rule-out\" or \"probable\" diagnosis in the outpatient setting, code the symptom instead; sequence the **primary diagnosis** first; and watch **Excludes1** notes, which forbid two codes together."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Position","S42.321A","Meaning"],rows:[["1 (letter)","S","Chapter: injury, poisoning"],["2–3 (digits)","42","Category: fracture of shoulder and upper arm"],[".","",". separates category from detail"],["4","3","Shaft of humerus"],["5","2","Displaced transverse fracture"],["6","1","Right arm (laterality)"],["7","A","Initial encounter, closed fracture"]]},
  terms:[["ICD-10-CM","US diagnosis code set."],["Category","First three characters of the code."],["Laterality","Right, left or bilateral, built into the code."],["Seventh character","Encounter type for injuries: A initial, D subsequent, S sequela."],["Placeholder X","Fills empty positions so a seventh character can be used."],["Highest specificity","Use the most detailed code the documentation supports."],["Excludes1","Two codes that may never be reported together."],["ICD-10-PCS","The separate inpatient hospital procedure code set used on UB-04 claims."]],
  remember:"Codes change every 1 October. A valid code on 30 September can be invalid on 1 October and deny as \"invalid diagnosis code\"."},
 {id:"cpt-hcpcs",title:"CPT and HCPCS: procedure and service codes",
  line:"CPT codes are five-digit numeric codes from the AMA for procedures and services. HCPCS Level II codes are a letter plus four digits for supplies, drugs, equipment and services CPT does not cover.",
  body:[
   "**CPT (Current Procedural Terminology)** is developed and maintained by the American Medical Association (AMA) and updated every 1 January. CPT is also called HCPCS Level I. Codes are **five characters, almost always numeric**, for example 99214 (established patient office visit, moderate complexity), 71020 (chest X-ray, now replaced by 71046), 29305 (hip spica cast) and 77295 (3-D radiation simulation).",
   "**Three categories.** **Category I**: the main set, five digits, grouped into Evaluation and Management (99202–99499), Anesthesia (00100–01999), Surgery (10004–69990), Radiology (70010–79999), Pathology and Laboratory (80047–89398) and Medicine (90281–99607). **Category II**: four digits followed by **F** (such as 3074F), optional tracking codes for performance measurement; they carry no payment. **Category III**: four digits followed by **T** (such as 0042T), temporary codes for emerging technology and procedures.",
   "**HCPCS Level II** (Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System) is maintained by CMS: a **letter followed by four digits**, for example A6234 (hydrocolloid dressing), L5818 (prosthetic knee component), J1885 (ketorolac injection), E0601 (CPAP device), G0439 (Medicare annual wellness visit). They cover durable medical equipment, supplies, drugs administered in the office, ambulance, and Medicare-specific services.",
   "**Evaluation and Management (E/M)** codes are the most common codes in physician billing. Since 2021 the office visit level (99202–99215) is chosen by medical decision making or total time, and the new-versus-established distinction decides which range applies. **NCCI edits** (National Correct Coding Initiative) define code pairs that cannot be billed together without a modifier, the source of bundling denials."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Set","Format","Owner","Examples","Used for"],rows:[["CPT Category I","5 digits","AMA","99214, 36415, 71046","Procedures and services"],["CPT Category II","4 digits + F","AMA","3074F, 1036F","Performance tracking (no payment)"],["CPT Category III","4 digits + T","AMA","0042T","Emerging technology"],["HCPCS Level II","Letter + 4 digits","CMS","A6234, J1885, E0601, G0439","Supplies, drugs, DME, Medicare services"]]},
  terms:[["CPT","Current Procedural Terminology, AMA's procedure code set (HCPCS Level I)."],["HCPCS Level II","CMS codes for supplies, drugs, DME and other services."],["Category I / II / III","Main codes; F performance codes; T emerging-technology codes."],["E/M codes","Evaluation and management: office, hospital and consult visit codes."],["NCCI edits","CMS code-pair rules behind bundling denials."],["Units","Number of times a code was performed; J-codes bill by dosage units."],["RVU","Relative value unit: the weight CMS assigns a code to compute its fee."]],
  remember:"A code is just a label until the payer accepts it. Check age, sex and frequency edits, and whether the payer wants the CPT or the HCPCS version of the same service."},
 {id:"modifiers",title:"Modifiers: pricing and informational",
  line:"A modifier is a two-character code appended to a procedure code to report that the service was altered in some way without changing its definition. Pricing modifiers change the payment; informational modifiers add detail.",
  body:[
   "**Modifiers** are two digits, two letters or a mix, written after the CPT or HCPCS code with a hyphen (99214-25). They explain circumstances such as a repeat service, a bilateral procedure, a professional-only component or which side of the body. A claim can carry up to four modifiers per line; the pricing modifier goes first.",
   "**Pricing (payment) modifiers** change how much is paid: 22 increased procedural service; 26 professional component; TC technical component; 50 bilateral procedure; 51 multiple procedures; 52 reduced services; 53 discontinued procedure; 54, 55 and 56 surgical care split; 62 two surgeons; 66 surgical team; 80, 81, 82 and AS assistant surgeon. These are sometimes called CPT modifiers.",
   "**Informational (statistical) modifiers** give detail that supports payment but does not price the line: 24 unrelated E/M in the global period; 25 significant, separately identifiable E/M on the same day as a procedure; 57 decision for surgery; 59 distinct procedural service (and its specific versions XE, XP, XS, XU); 76 repeat procedure by the same provider; 77 repeat procedure by another provider; 78 and 79 return to the operating room; LT and RT left and right side; GA, GY and GZ for Medicare waiver status; and anatomical modifiers such as F1 to F9 for fingers and T1 to T9 for toes. HCPCS Level II modifiers are the alphabetic ones.",
   "Modifiers are the fix for several denial scenarios in this app: 77 when two providers performed the same service on the same day, 59 or the X modifiers when an NCCI bundling edit allows separation, 25 when an E/M is bundled into a procedure, 50 or LT/RT when a bilateral service denies as duplicate."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Modifier","Meaning","Type"],rows:[["22","Increased procedural service","Pricing"],["24","Unrelated E/M during a post-operative period","Informational"],["25","Significant, separately identifiable E/M same day as a procedure","Informational"],["26","Professional component (reading/interpretation)","Pricing"],["TC","Technical component (equipment, technician)","Pricing"],["50","Bilateral procedure","Pricing"],["51","Multiple procedures same session","Pricing"],["57","Decision for surgery","Informational"],["59","Distinct procedural service","Informational"],["76","Repeat procedure, same provider","Informational"],["77","Repeat procedure, different provider","Informational"],["LT / RT","Left side / right side","Informational"],["GA","Waiver of liability (ABN) on file","Informational"],["GY","Statutorily excluded service","Informational"]]},
  terms:[["Modifier","Two-character addition to a procedure code."],["Pricing modifier","Changes the payment amount; listed first on the line."],["Informational modifier","Adds detail without changing the price."],["Professional vs technical component","26 pays the physician's interpretation; TC pays the facility's equipment and staff; no modifier pays both (global)."],["Global period","Days after a surgery during which related care is included in the surgical fee (0, 10 or 90 days)."],["X modifiers","XE, XP, XS, XU: more specific alternatives to 59."]],
  remember:"Use a modifier only when the documentation supports the circumstance it describes. Adding 59 to get past an edit without support is a false claim."},
 {id:"charge-entry",step:5,title:"Demographic entry and charge entry",
  line:"Demographic entry creates or updates the patient's account from the facesheet. Charge entry posts the coded services from the superbill onto that account so a claim can be generated.",
  body:[
   "**Demographic (demo) entry** is entering a new patient's personal information from the facesheet into the client's practice management software: name, DOB, sex, SSN, address, phone, guarantor information and insurance information (payer, member ID, group, subscriber, relationship, effective dates). An established patient's account is updated for any change.",
   "**Charge entry** (charge capture) is entering the encounter details from the superbill: patient, rendering and referring provider, date of service, place of service, ICD-10-CM codes, CPT/HCPCS codes with modifiers and units, the diagnosis pointers linking each line to its diagnosis, the charge amount from the fee schedule, and the authorisation number if one exists.",
   "Charge entry is the last human check before the claim leaves. The team confirms the patient was found correctly (name and DOB, not name alone), the insurance on the account matches the card for that DOS, the location and type of service agree with the codes, and the charges balance to the superbill. Batches are totalled and reconciled so that no encounter is missed or entered twice.",
   "Common errors caught here: wrong patient selected, DOS typed in the wrong month, inactive insurance attached, missing modifier, units left at zero, charge posted to the wrong provider or location. Each one is a rejection or denial avoided."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Claim submission",steps:["Charge entry team receives the coded information, charge ticket or superbill","Patient is searched in the system by name and date of birth; demographics are created or updated","Location, type of service, date of service, codes and charges are verified and entered in the client software","Final check is completed before the claim is forwarded for billing and claims processing"]},
  terms:[["Demographic entry","Creating or updating the patient account from the facesheet."],["Charge entry / charge capture","Posting coded services and charges to the account."],["Practice management system (PMS)","The billing software holding accounts, charges, claims and payments."],["Fee schedule (provider)","The provider's list of charges by code."],["Batch","A group of encounters entered and reconciled together."],["Charge lag","Days between the DOS and charge entry; long lags threaten timely filing."]],
  remember:"Search by name and date of birth, never name alone. Posting to the wrong patient is a HIPAA problem and a billing problem at once."}
]},
{id:"m6",short:"Claims & adjudication",title:"Claims: submission to adjudication",day:"Day 2",
 blurb:"The claim form, the clearinghouse that scrubs it, and the payer that decides to pay it.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"claim-forms",step:6,title:"Claim forms: CMS-1500 and UB-04",
  line:"Professional services go on the CMS-1500 (electronic 837P); facility and institutional services go on the UB-04 (electronic 837I). Almost all claims are sent electronically through a clearinghouse.",
  body:[
   "A **claim** is the standard document a provider submits to a payer to request reimbursement. Two paper forms, and their electronic equivalents, cover US healthcare.",
   "The **CMS-1500** (red form, maintained by the National Uniform Claim Committee) is for **professional** claims: physicians, nurse practitioners, therapists, labs, ambulance, DME suppliers. Key boxes: 1a insured's ID; 2 patient name; 3 DOB and sex; 4 insured's name; 6 relationship; 11 group number; 17 referring/ordering provider and 17b their NPI; 21 up to twelve ICD-10-CM codes; 23 prior authorisation number; 24A DOS; 24B POS; 24D CPT/HCPCS and modifiers; 24E diagnosis pointer; 24F charge; 24G units; 24J rendering NPI; 25 tax ID; 26 patient account number; 28 total charge; 31 provider signature; 32 service facility; 33 billing provider and 33a billing NPI. Electronically it is the **837P**.",
   "The **UB-04** (CMS-1450) is for **institutional** claims: hospitals, SNFs, home health, hospice, outpatient facilities. It uses form locators instead of boxes: type of bill (FL4, such as 0111 inpatient or 0131 outpatient), admission and discharge dates, patient status, **revenue codes** (FL42, such as 0450 emergency room), HCPCS/CPT where required, and ICD-10-CM diagnoses plus ICD-10-PCS procedures for inpatient stays. Electronically it is the **837I**. Hospitals bill the facility charge on a UB-04 and the physicians bill their professional charges on CMS-1500s for the same stay.",
   "Other HIPAA transactions you will hear about: **835** electronic remittance advice (the ERA), **270/271** eligibility, **276/277** claim status inquiry and response, **278** authorisation request, and **999 / 277CA** acknowledgments from the clearinghouse and payer."
  ],
  compare:{head:["","CMS-1500 / 837P","UB-04 / 837I"],rows:[["Who bills","Physicians, practitioners, suppliers","Hospitals, facilities, home health, hospice"],["Diagnosis codes","ICD-10-CM","ICD-10-CM (+ ICD-10-PCS inpatient procedures)"],["Service codes","CPT / HCPCS with modifiers","Revenue codes, plus CPT/HCPCS for outpatient"],["Place of service","2-digit POS code","Type of bill code"],["Rendering identifier","Rendering NPI (24J)","Attending NPI (FL76)"]]},
  terms:[["Claim","Standard request for payment sent to a payer."],["CMS-1500","Paper professional claim form; 837P electronically."],["UB-04 / CMS-1450","Paper institutional claim form; 837I electronically."],["Revenue code","4-digit UB-04 code for the department or type of service."],["Type of bill","UB-04 code for facility type, bill classification and frequency."],["Payer ID","5-character identifier routing an electronic claim to the right payer."],["Patient account number","Provider's internal account reference, box 26, returned on the remittance."]],
  remember:"Box 24J rendering NPI, box 33a billing NPI and box 25 tax ID must all match the payer's enrollment record. Most \"provider not eligible\" denials trace to one of those three."},
 {id:"clearinghouse",step:7,title:"Claim transmission, the clearinghouse and rejections",
  line:"The billing organisation sends claims electronically to a clearinghouse, which scrubs them for errors, rejects the bad ones back for correction, and forwards clean claims to each payer.",
  body:[
   "After charge entry the practice management system generates the claim file. The **clearinghouse** (Availity, Change Healthcare, Waystar, Trizetto and others) receives it, converts it to each payer's required format, runs **scrubber edits** (missing fields, invalid codes, code and age or sex mismatches, payer-specific rules), and transmits the clean claims to the payers. In return it sends back acknowledgment and status reports.",
   "A **rejection** is a claim that never entered the payer's adjudication system: the clearinghouse or the payer's front-end edits refused it for invalid or incomplete data. It has no claim number at the payer and does not count toward timely filing. A **denial** is a claim the payer accepted, processed and decided not to pay; it has a claim number and an EOB with a reason code. Rejections are fixed and resubmitted; denials are corrected or appealed.",
   "The **scrubber report** (clearinghouse report) tells the provider how many claims were received, how many dropped or rejected for incomplete or incorrect information and why, and how many were sent on to the payers. Working it daily is a core billing task, because a rejected claim sitting unnoticed is the most common way to blow a filing limit.",
   "Paper claims are still used for a few payers and for attachments, and are keyed or scanned at the payer, so they take weeks longer to process."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Insurance adjudication",steps:["Practice system generates claims; the system kicks out claims with invalid information","Claim edits are corrected","Claims are transmitted electronically to the clearinghouse","The clearinghouse report is reviewed; rejections are fixed and resent","Clean claims are submitted electronically to the insurance company, which acknowledges receipt"]},
  terms:[["Clearinghouse","Intermediary that scrubs, formats and transmits claims between providers and payers."],["Scrubbing","Automated edit checks before submission."],["Rejection","Claim refused before adjudication for data errors; no payer claim number."],["Denial","Claim adjudicated and not paid; has a claim number and reason codes."],["Scrubber report","Clearinghouse summary of claims received, rejected and forwarded."],["999 / 277CA","Acknowledgment transactions confirming a file and each claim were accepted."],["Proof of timely filing","Clearinghouse acceptance report showing the payer received the claim within the limit."]],
  remember:"A rejected claim was never received by the payer. Fix it today; the filing clock never stopped."},
 {id:"adjudication",step:8,title:"Insurance adjudication: paid, denied, partially paid, pended",
  line:"Adjudication is the payer's process of applying the member's benefits and its own rules to the claim and deciding what it will pay. The decision comes back as an EOB or ERA with payment, or with reason codes explaining why not.",
  body:[
   "**Claims adjudication** is the determination of the insurer's payment or financial responsibility after the member's insurance benefits are applied to a medical claim, following the payer's standard guidelines. The payer screens the claim (is the member eligible, is the provider recognised, is the claim complete), adjudicates it (is the service covered, authorised, medically necessary, coded correctly, within filing limits, and what is the allowed amount and the patient's share), and communicates the decision.",
   "**Four outcomes.** **Paid**: approved and paid, in full at the allowed amount. **Denied**: refused, with a claim adjustment reason code (CARC) and often a remittance advice remark code (RARC) explaining why. **Partially paid**: some lines paid, some denied, or paid at a reduced amount. **No response / pended**: the payer is still processing, has requested more information, or never received the claim; after the normal processing time (typically 30 to 45 days) it is worked as a no-response claim.",
   "**What comes back.** The **EOB (Explanation of Benefits)**, a statement sent to the provider and patient explaining what was paid and denied, commonly attached to a paper cheque. The **ERA (Electronic Remittance Advice, the 835)**, the same information in a standard computer-readable format that posts automatically. **Correspondence**: letters requesting records, notifying of audits, or explaining decisions. And the payment itself: a **cheque**, a virtual credit card, or an **EFT** (electronic funds transfer) to the provider's bank.",
   "Reason codes are grouped: **CO** contractual obligation (provider absorbs), **PR** patient responsibility, **OA** other adjustment, **PI** payer-initiated reduction. CO-45 is the contractual adjustment; PR-1, PR-2 and PR-3 are deductible, coinsurance and copay; CO-97 bundling, CO-29 timely filing, CO-197 no authorisation and so on. The denial scenarios in the Ask tab are organised around these codes."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Payment posting",steps:["Insurance receives and reviews the claim","Adjudication is performed against standard payer guidelines: paid, denied, partially paid, or additional information required","The documented response is sent to the provider and patient as an EOB or ERA","Payment is issued by cheque or electronic funds transfer"]},
  terms:[["Adjudication","Payer's decision process on a claim."],["Paid / denied / partially paid / pended","The four adjudication outcomes."],["EOB","Explanation of Benefits: human-readable statement of the decision."],["ERA (835)","Electronic Remittance Advice: machine-readable remittance."],["EFT","Electronic funds transfer of the payment."],["CARC","Claim Adjustment Reason Code: why an amount was not paid."],["RARC","Remittance Advice Remark Code: extra explanation."],["Group codes CO / PR / OA / PI","Who is responsible for an adjusted amount."],["Pended claim","Claim held by the payer awaiting information or review."]],
  remember:"Read the CARC and the RARC together, then check the group code. CO is yours to fix or absorb, PR is the patient's to pay."}
]},
{id:"m7",short:"Payments & AR",title:"Payments, AR and collections",day:"Day 2",
 blurb:"Posting what the payer sent, chasing what it did not, refunding what it overpaid, and collecting the patient's share.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"payment-posting",step:9,title:"Payment posting",
  line:"Payment posting records every payment, adjustment and denial from the EOB, ERA or cheque onto the right patient account and claim line, so balances are correct and the next step is clear.",
  body:[
   "Posting can be **manual** (reading the EOB and keying each line) or **electronic** (auto-posting the 835 ERA, with exceptions worked by hand). **Insurance payment posting** needs the payment details (cheque or EFT number, date, amount) and the EOB or ERA. **Patient payment posting** needs the cheque, card slip or EFT details for cash, cards and online payments.",
   "From each remittance line the poster records the billed amount, allowed amount, deductible, coinsurance and copay (PR), contractual adjustment (CO-45), other adjustments with their reason codes, and the paid amount, then moves any remaining balance to the next responsible party: the secondary payer or the patient. Denied lines are posted with their CARC and RARC so AR can see them.",
   "An **EOB or ERA contains**: the patient's basic details (name, DOB, claim number, insurance name), the rendering physician, DOS, POS, procedure code, modifier and units, billed amount, allowed amount, copay, deductible, contractual amount, coinsurance amount, paid amount, and the reason for any denial. A **cheque** carries the issuer, the receiver, the cheque number and the amount.",
   "**Batch reconciliation**: the total posted from a remittance must equal the cheque or EFT amount to the cent. Unapplied or unidentified cash is logged and researched, never left floating. Audits check for posting to the wrong patient, wrong line, or taking a contractual adjustment on a non-contracted payer."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Accounts receivable",steps:["Receipt of correspondence for posting: EOBs, cheques, ERAs and card payments","Insurance payments and denials are posted to the system line by line","Batches are reconciled: posted totals equal the deposit","Audits confirm correct entry of payments and adjustments","Reports are generated for AR and management"]},
  terms:[["Payment posting","Applying remittance amounts to accounts and claims."],["Auto-posting","Posting the 835 ERA automatically."],["Contractual adjustment","Billed minus allowed, written off (CO-45)."],["Unapplied cash","Money received but not yet matched to a claim."],["Batch reconciliation","Posted total equals deposited total."],["Secondary billing","Sending the balance to the next payer with the primary EOB."],["Take-back / recoupment","Payer recovering a prior payment by deducting it from a later remittance."]],
  remember:"Everything downstream depends on posting. A denial posted as a zero-pay with no reason code disappears from AR until it is too late to appeal."},
 {id:"ar-followup",step:10,title:"Accounts receivable follow-up",
  line:"Accounts receivable (AR) is the money owed to the practice for services rendered but not yet paid. AR follow-up finds out why each unpaid claim is unpaid and moves it toward payment.",
  body:[
   "**Accounts receivable** is the total amount owed to a practice for services rendered but not yet paid, by payers and by patients. It is the health indicator of the practice: how much, how old, and from whom. AR is reported in **aging buckets** (0–30, 31–60, 61–90, 91–120, over 120 days) by payer, and summarised as **days in AR** and the **percentage of AR over 90 days**.",
   "Every claim in AR has one of four statuses: **paid claim** (approved and paid; only the balance after payment remains, if any), **denied claim** (refused by the insurer), **partially paid claim** (paid in part), or **no-response claim** (sent, and nothing heard after the normal processing time). The AR team works the last three.",
   "**AR follow-up** means checking claim status on the payer portal or by phone, documenting what the payer says (received date, status, denial reason, what is needed, rep name, call reference), and taking the next action: resubmit, send a corrected claim, supply records, appeal, bill the secondary, move to patient responsibility, or adjust. The denial library in the Ask tab is the script for those calls."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Denial management and credit balance",steps:["AR analysis: aging reports identify outstanding claims by payer and age","AR reports are generated for the outstanding payments to be worked","AR follow-up with insurances by portal and phone; each claim's status is documented","Denied and underpaid claims go to denial management","Claims with a credit balance are transferred to the credit balance team"]},
  terms:[["Accounts receivable (AR)","Money owed for services rendered but not yet paid."],["Aging bucket","Grouping of AR by days since the DOS or billing date."],["Days in AR","Total AR divided by average daily charges."],["No-response claim","No payer response after normal processing time."],["Claim status","Where the payer says the claim is: received, in process, paid, denied."],["Call reference number","Payer's reference for a phone call; proof the follow-up happened."],["Insurance AR vs patient AR","Balances owed by payers versus by patients."]],
  tips:["Work the oldest high-dollar claims first, but never let a small claim pass its appeal deadline.","A no-response claim at 30 days gets a status check; at 45 days with no claim on file it gets resubmitted with proof of timely filing."],
  remember:"AR follow-up is a status question first and a denial question second: has the payer got it, what did they do with it, what do they need?"},
 {id:"denial-management",step:11,title:"Denial management",
  line:"Denial management recovers the money on denied claims by correcting, resubmitting or appealing them, and then fixes the root causes so the same denial stops happening.",
  body:[
   "A denial is the payer saying no with a reason code. Denial management has two halves. **Recovery**: read the CARC and RARC, call the payer if the reason is unclear, decide between a corrected claim (frequency code 7), a reconsideration or a formal **appeal** with records, and act within the payer's deadline. **Prevention**: track denials by reason, payer and provider, find the pattern (a front desk missing authorisations, a coder omitting modifier 25, a payer that needs a different taxonomy), and fix the process.",
   "Denials fall into recognisable families: eligibility and coverage (not eligible, terminated, other payer primary), authorisation and referral, timely filing, coding and bundling (inconsistent diagnosis, NCCI, modifiers), duplicate claims, credentialing and provider enrolment, medical necessity and non-covered services, and payment or status issues (applied to deductible, paid to patient). The Ask tab covers 66 of these scenarios with the exact questions to ask the payer and the steps to take.",
   "Measures: **denial rate** (denied claims as a share of submitted, target under 5 to 10%), **overturn rate** (appealed denials that paid), and **preventable denial percentage**. Every denial that is written off is revenue the practice earned and lost."
  ],
  terms:[["Denial management","Recovering denied claims and preventing their recurrence."],["Corrected claim","Resubmission with frequency code 7 and the original claim number, fixing an error."],["Appeal","Formal written request to overturn a denial, with supporting records, within the payer's deadline."],["Reconsideration","First-level, often informal, review request."],["Denial rate","Share of claims denied."],["Root cause","The upstream process failure behind a denial pattern."],["Write-off (bad debt / timely filing)","Denied amounts the practice could not recover."]],
  tips:["Open the Ask tab and type the denial code or the payer's wording; the scenario gives you the call script and the next step.","Log the denial reason for every denied line so the monthly denial report is real."],
  remember:"Work the claim, then fix the cause. A denial appealed and won once will come back every month until the process changes."},
 {id:"credit-balance",step:12,title:"Credit balance",
  line:"A credit balance is an account where payments and allowances exceed the total charges. It is money that belongs to someone else, usually a payer or the patient, and must be refunded or corrected promptly.",
  body:[
   "**Credit balance**: accounts in the system where payments or adjustments exceed the total charge amount, leaving a negative balance. Causes include **patient overpayment** (copay collected when none was due, or paid before the payer's share was known), **insurance overpayment** (paid above the allowed amount, or paid as primary when secondary), **insurance paid twice** (duplicate payment on a resubmitted claim), **patient paid twice**, and **COB conflicts** (two payers each paying as primary). Posting errors, such as a payment on the wrong account, create false credits.",
   "Credit balances are a compliance matter, not just housekeeping. Medicare overpayments must be reported and returned within 60 days of identification under the Affordable Care Act's \"60-day rule\"; keeping a known overpayment is a false claim. States have unclaimed property laws for patient refunds that cannot be delivered.",
   "The credit balance team reviews each account, identifies the reason, and resolves it: a refund to the payer or patient, a reversal of a wrong posting, moving a payment to the correct account or DOS, or a recoupment request to the payer. Accounts that still carry a patient balance after the credit is cleared go to patient collections."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Patient collections",steps:["Credit balance team works the accounts showing a credit (negative) balance","The reason for each credit is identified: overpayment, duplicate payment, COB conflict or posting error","Refunds are processed where applicable; posting errors are reversed","Accounts with pending patient payments are transferred to the patient collection department"]},
  terms:[["Credit balance","Payments plus adjustments greater than charges."],["Overpayment","Payment above what was due."],["Refund","Returning money to the payer or patient."],["Recoupment","Payer recovering an overpayment from future remittances."],["60-day rule","Medicare overpayments must be reported and returned within 60 days of identification."],["Unclaimed property","State law for refunds that cannot be delivered to the patient."]],
  remember:"A credit is not revenue. Resolve it within the payer's and the law's timelines, and document every refund."},
 {id:"patient-collections",step:13,title:"Patient collections",
  line:"After the payers have paid, whatever the policy makes the patient responsible for is billed to the patient through statements, and, if unpaid, referred to a collection agency.",
  body:[
   "Per the policy, the patient is liable for a certain amount: copay, deductible, coinsurance, non-covered services and, for out-of-network care where allowed, balance bills. After the insurance payment posts, any amount due from the patient must be collected. If the provider cannot collect it, the account is referred to a collection agency.",
   "A **patient statement** is the document the provider sends the patient when payment is due: practice name and address, patient and guarantor, account number, dates of service, services, charges, payments and adjustments, the balance and the amount due, plus ways to pay. The billing organisation generates and mails or emails it on the provider's behalf and follows up with the patient. Practices send a cycle of statements (commonly three, 30 days apart) and may call between them.",
   "A **collection agency** is a third party hired by the provider to pursue unpaid patient balances through letters (**dunning** or demand letters) and calls, within the limits of the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act. Agencies keep a percentage of what they collect, and accounts sent to them are written off the practice's AR as **bad debt**. Before referral the practice confirms the balance is truly the patient's: insurance has paid correctly, no secondary exists, and no financial assistance or payment plan applies.",
   "Good practice reduces collections: collect copays and known deductibles at check-in, give estimates up front (the No Surprises Act requires good-faith estimates for uninsured and self-pay patients), offer payment plans and online payment, and screen for charity care."
  ],
  flow:{to:"Completion of the cycle",steps:["Patient statement is generated by the provider through the billing organisation","Responses are reviewed; if there is no response, further statements are sent per the practice's policy","If there is still no response, the patient is followed up by the collection team or referred to a collection agency","Balance is paid, placed on a payment plan, adjusted, or written off as bad debt"]},
  terms:[["Patient responsibility","Amounts the policy assigns to the patient."],["Patient statement","Bill sent to the patient showing services, payments and amount due."],["Dunning letter","Demand letter in a collection sequence."],["Collection agency","Third party pursuing unpaid patient balances for a fee."],["Bad debt","Patient balances deemed uncollectible and written off."],["Payment plan","Agreed instalments for a patient balance."],["Good-faith estimate","Advance cost estimate required for uninsured and self-pay patients."]],
  remember:"Confirm the balance is really the patient's before the first statement goes out. Billing a patient for a payer's error is the fastest way to lose both the money and the patient."}
]},
{id:"m8",short:"US payers",title:"Insurance in the US: the payers",day:"Day 3",
 blurb:"Who actually pays the claims: government programs, commercial insurers, and liability carriers, with the rules that make each one different.",
 lessons:[
 {id:"federal-overview",title:"Federal and state programs: the map",
  line:"Government programs cover the elderly, the poor, the military, veterans, children, federal employees and tribal members. Together they make government the largest single payer in the US.",
  body:[
   "Federal insurance is run and funded by the US government to provide healthcare benefits to older citizens, retirees, people with disabilities, low-income families, children, military members and veterans, and government employees. The **Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)**, part of the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), runs Medicare, sets the national rules for Medicaid and CHIP, and decides which services and technologies Medicare covers and how much it pays. The Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) sets quality and safety standards.",
   "The programs: **1. Medicare** (65+, disabled, ESRD). **2. Medicaid** (low income; state-run with federal rules). **3. TRICARE** (Department of Defense Military Health System, formerly CHAMPUS). **4. Veterans Health Administration (VHA)** and **CHAMPVA** for veterans and certain dependents. **5. Children's Health Insurance Program (CHIP)**. **6. Federal Employees Health Benefits (FEHB)** program. **7. Indian Health Service (IHS)**. **8. Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE)**. Plus **Railroad Medicare**, administered by the Railroad Retirement Board for railroad workers.",
   "Government payers behave differently from commercial ones: their rules are public and uniform (Medicare's are the same in every state), their fee schedules are published, they prohibit or tightly limit balance billing, they carry strict fraud and abuse laws (False Claims Act, Anti-Kickback Statute, Stark Law), and their IDs follow fixed formats you can recognise on sight."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Program","Who it covers","Run by","Billing note"],rows:[["Medicare","65+, disabled 24+ months, ESRD, ALS","CMS via regional MACs","Uniform national rules; 11-character MBI"],["Medicaid","Low-income individuals and families","Each state, within federal rules","Always payer of last resort; verify monthly"],["TRICARE","Active duty, retirees, families","DoD / Defense Health Agency","East (Humana Military) and West (TriWest) regions"],["VHA / CHAMPVA","Veterans; dependents of disabled or deceased veterans","Department of Veterans Affairs","Community care needs VA authorisation"],["CHIP","Children in families above Medicaid limits","States with federal funding","Often administered with Medicaid"],["FEHB","Federal employees and retirees","Office of Personnel Management","Commercial carriers (BCBS FEP, GEHA)"],["IHS","Members of federally recognised tribes","HHS","Direct care plus purchased/referred care"],["PACE","Frail elderly 55+ needing nursing-home level care","Local PACE organisations","Capitated; PACE pays all care"]]},
  terms:[["CMS","Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services."],["HHS","Department of Health and Human Services, CMS's parent."],["MAC","Medicare Administrative Contractor: the regional company that processes Medicare claims (Novitas, Noridian, Palmetto GBA and others)."],["False Claims Act","Federal law penalising knowingly false claims to government programs."],["Anti-Kickback Statute / Stark Law","Laws against paying for referrals and self-referral to owned entities."]],
  remember:"Government payers publish their rules. When a Medicare claim denies, the answer is in a CMS manual or the MAC's local coverage determination, not in a negotiation."},
 {id:"medicare",title:"Medicare: Parts A, B, C and D",
  line:"Medicare is the federal program for people 65 and over, people disabled for more than two years, and people with end-stage renal disease or ALS. It has four parts and its own vocabulary.",
  body:[
   "**Eligibility**: age 65 or older; under 65 and receiving Social Security disability benefits for 24 months; any age with **end-stage renal disease (ESRD)** needing dialysis or transplant; or diagnosed with **ALS** (coverage begins immediately). Medicare is the biggest federal insurance, its rules are the same across the US, and it is governed by CMS, with claims processed by regional Medicare Administrative Contractors (MACs).",
   "**Part A (Hospital Insurance)** covers inpatient hospital stays, skilled nursing facility care after a qualifying stay, hospice and home health. Most people pay no premium. **Part B (Medical Insurance)** covers physician services, outpatient hospital care, ambulatory surgery, lab tests, durable medical equipment (DME), preventive services and drugs administered in the office. It has a monthly premium ($202.90 standard in 2026). **Part C (Medicare Advantage)** is Original Medicare delivered through a private managed care plan (Humana, UnitedHealthcare, Aetna and others), usually with Part D included and network rules; claims go to the plan, not to Medicare. **Part D** is prescription drug coverage through private plans.",
   "**Cost sharing (2026)**: Part A deductible $1,736 per benefit period, with daily coinsurance of $434 for hospital days 61–90 and $868 for lifetime reserve days, and $217 per day for SNF days 21–100. Part B deductible $283 per year, after which Medicare pays **80% of the fee schedule amount and the patient owes 20%** coinsurance. These figures change every January; the deck's 2017 numbers were $1,316 and $183.",
   "**Medicare ID.** Since 2020 every beneficiary has a **Medicare Beneficiary Identifier (MBI)**: 11 characters, letters and numbers, no SSN, printed on a red, white and blue card, for example 1EG4-TE5-MK73. The old HICN format (nine-digit SSN plus a letter suffix such as A, B or C2) is no longer accepted. **Railroad Medicare** beneficiaries have the Railroad Retirement Board logo on the card and their claims go to the Railroad Medicare contractor (Palmetto GBA), not the local MAC.",
   "**Medigap (Medicare Supplement)** policies, sold by private companies and lettered Plan A to Plan N, pay the copays, coinsurance and deductibles Original Medicare leaves to the patient; they are always secondary and most receive claims automatically by **crossover**. **Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP)** describes the situations where another insurer must pay first (employer plan for an actively working beneficiary at a 20+ employee firm, workers' compensation, no-fault or liability). An **Advance Beneficiary Notice (ABN)** is a written notice given to the patient before a service that Medicare may not cover, making the patient responsible if it is denied; modifier GA on the claim says an ABN is on file."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Part","Covers","Premium","Patient pays (2026)"],rows:[["A","Inpatient hospital, SNF after a 3-day stay, hospice, home health","Usually none","$1,736 deductible per benefit period; daily coinsurance after day 60"],["B","Physician, outpatient, ASC, lab, DME, preventive, office drugs","$202.90/month standard","$283 annual deductible, then 20% coinsurance"],["C","Medicare Advantage: A + B (usually D) through a private plan","Varies by plan","Plan copays; network rules apply"],["D","Prescription drugs through private plans","Varies by plan","Plan deductible and copays; $2,100 out-of-pocket cap in 2026"]]},
  terms:[["Medicare","Federal program for 65+, long-term disabled, ESRD and ALS."],["Original Medicare","Parts A and B administered directly by CMS."],["Medicare Advantage (Part C)","Private plan replacing Original Medicare for enrolled members."],["Medicare Beneficiary Identifier (MBI)","11-character Medicare ID, replaced the SSN-based HICN."],["Benefit period","Part A deductible period: starts at admission, ends 60 days after discharge."],["Medigap","Private supplement paying Medicare cost sharing."],["Crossover","Medicare forwarding the claim to the secondary automatically."],["Medicare Secondary Payer (MSP)","Rules for when another payer is primary to Medicare."],["Advance Beneficiary Notice (ABN)","Pre-service notice making the patient liable if Medicare denies."],["Local coverage determination (LCD)","MAC policy on when a service is covered."]],
  tips:["A Medicare Advantage card means bill the plan, not Medicare; eligibility checks on the Medicare portal show the MA plan's name.","Part B pays 80% of the allowed amount, so a 20% PR-2 line on every Medicare EOB is normal, not a denial."],
  remember:"MBI, not SSN. Part A inpatient, Part B physician and outpatient, Part C private plan, Part D drugs. 80:20 after the deductible."},
 {id:"medicaid",title:"Medicaid",
  line:"Medicaid is the joint federal and state program for people with low income. Each state runs its own version, eligibility can change month to month, and Medicaid is always the payer of last resort.",
  body:[
   "Medicaid is funded jointly by the federal government and the states and administered by each state within CMS rules, so benefits, provider enrolment, fee schedules and even the program's name vary (Medi-Cal in California, MassHealth in Massachusetts, TennCare in Tennessee). It covers people and families with low income, pregnant women, children, people with disabilities and many nursing-home residents; states that expanded under the ACA cover all adults up to 138% of the federal poverty level.",
   "**Monthly enrollment.** Eligibility is re-determined often, so coverage can start or stop at the beginning of any month. Verify Medicaid eligibility for every DOS, every month. **Medicaid spend-down** (medically needy): a person whose income is above the limit becomes eligible once medical expenses reduce the remaining income below the threshold for that period.",
   "**Benefits** typically include primary care visits and check-ups, health screenings (blood tests, X-rays), yearly exams, birth control and full pregnancy care, emergency care, complete medical care for infants and children (EPSDT), dental, vision and hearing care (mostly for children), home health care, and long-term care. Many states deliver Medicaid through **managed care organisations (MCOs)**, so the card may show a commercial name (Molina, Centene, Amerigroup) and the claim goes to the MCO.",
   "**Billing rules.** Medicaid is always the **last payer**: every other coverage, including Medicare, pays first. Providers must be enrolled with the state Medicaid program; **balance billing a Medicaid patient is prohibited**, and the Medicaid allowed amount is usually the lowest of any payer. The member ID has no national pattern; each state issues its own card (California BIC, New York Benefit Identification Card, New Jersey Health Benefits ID). Timely filing limits are often short and strict."
  ],
  terms:[["Medicaid","Federal-state program for low-income people."],["Medicaid expansion","ACA option covering adults up to 138% of the federal poverty level."],["Spend-down / medically needy","Qualifying for Medicaid after medical expenses reduce income below the limit."],["Managed care organisation (MCO)","Private plan administering Medicaid benefits for the state."],["Payer of last resort","Medicaid pays only after all other coverage."],["EPSDT","Early and Periodic Screening, Diagnostic and Treatment: Medicaid's comprehensive child benefit."],["Dual eligible","Patient with both Medicare and Medicaid; Medicare is primary."]],
  remember:"Medicaid last, Medicaid monthly, Medicaid never balance-billed."},
 {id:"military-other-federal",title:"TRICARE, VA, CHIP, FEHB, IHS and PACE",
  line:"Six smaller government programs each cover a specific population, and each has one or two rules a biller must know.",
  body:[
   "**TRICARE** is the Department of Defense Military Health System program, formerly the Civilian Health and Medical Program of the Uniformed Services (CHAMPUS). It covers active-duty service members, National Guard and Reserve members, military retirees, and their families and survivors. Since 2018 the US is split into two regions: **TRICARE East**, administered by **Humana Military**, and **TRICARE West**, administered by **TriWest Healthcare Alliance** from 1 January 2025 (previously Health Net). Plans include TRICARE Prime (HMO-like, PCM required), TRICARE Select (PPO-like) and **TRICARE For Life**, which is secondary to Medicare for retirees over 65. Overseas regions are handled separately.",
   "**Veterans Health Administration (VHA)** provides care to enrolled veterans directly through VA medical centres, with priority for service-connected disabilities. Veterans may receive **community care** from non-VA providers only with a VA referral and authorisation; those claims go to the VA's third-party administrator. **CHAMPVA** (Civilian Health and Medical Program of the Department of Veterans Affairs) covers spouses and children of veterans who are permanently and totally disabled from a service-connected condition or who died from one; it is not TRICARE and is secondary to Medicare.",
   "**Children's Health Insurance Program (CHIP)**, administered by states under HHS, covers uninsured children up to age 19 in families with income too high for Medicaid but too low for private coverage, and in some states pregnant women. Benefits: routine check-ups, immunisations, doctor visits, prescriptions, dental and vision, inpatient and outpatient hospital care, laboratory and X-ray services, and emergency services. Many states run CHIP inside their Medicaid program.",
   "**Federal Employees Health Benefits (FEHB)**, administered by the Office of Personnel Management (OPM), lets insurance companies, employee associations and unions offer plans to federal employees and retirees; BCBS Federal Employee Program (ID prefix R) and GEHA are the largest. FEHB can supplement Medicare for retirees by paying what Medicare leaves, though it is not a Medigap plan.",
   "**Indian Health Service (IHS)** provides medical and public health services to members of federally recognised tribes and Alaska Natives, through IHS and tribal facilities and through purchased/referred care from outside providers. **PACE (Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly)** is a Medicare and Medicaid program helping frail elderly people (55 or older, certified as needing nursing-home level care, able to live safely in the community with PACE support) stay at home; the PACE organisation receives a capitated payment and covers all care, including Part D drugs, so outside providers bill PACE, not Medicare."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Program","Who","Key billing rule"],rows:[["TRICARE","Military members, retirees, families","East = Humana Military, West = TriWest; TRICARE For Life is secondary to Medicare"],["VHA community care","Enrolled veterans referred outside the VA","Needs a VA authorisation number; bill the VA's administrator"],["CHAMPVA","Dependents of permanently disabled or deceased veterans","Secondary to Medicare; not TRICARE"],["CHIP","Children under 19 above Medicaid income limits","Often processed by the state Medicaid system"],["FEHB","Federal employees and retirees","Commercial carriers; BCBS FEP prefix R"],["IHS","Tribal members and Alaska Natives","Outside care needs a purchased/referred care authorisation"],["PACE","Frail elderly 55+","PACE pays all care; do not bill Medicare or Part D"]]},
  terms:[["TRICARE","DoD health program; East and West regions."],["TRICARE For Life","Medicare wraparound for military retirees 65+."],["CHAMPUS","TRICARE's former name."],["CHAMPVA","VA program for dependents of disabled or deceased veterans."],["Community care","Non-VA care authorised and paid by the VA."],["CHIP","Children's Health Insurance Program."],["FEHB","Federal Employees Health Benefits program (OPM)."],["IHS","Indian Health Service."],["PACE","Program of All-inclusive Care for the Elderly."]],
  remember:"Military and veteran coverage looks alike on the card but routes differently: TRICARE to Humana Military or TriWest, VA care only with a VA authorisation, CHAMPVA to the VA's CHAMPVA office."},
 {id:"commercial-payers",title:"Commercial payers and how to read their cards",
  line:"Commercial insurers sell individual and group plans. The big national names are Blue Cross Blue Shield, UnitedHealthcare, Aetna, Cigna and Humana, and each card tells you where to send the claim if you know how to read it.",
  body:[
   "**Commercial health insurance** is coverage sold by private companies, individually or through employer groups, categorised by renewal provisions and benefit type. The largest: **Blue Cross Blue Shield (BCBS)**, a federation of independent licensees (Anthem, Horizon BCBS of New Jersey, Premera, Empire, Florida Blue, Highmark and others); **UnitedHealthcare (UHC)**, part of UnitedHealth Group, the largest single insurer; **Aetna** (owned by CVS Health); **Cigna**; **Humana** (mostly Medicare Advantage); plus regional and specialty payers such as Kaiser Permanente, Molina, Centene and Oscar. Liberty Mutual, Travelers and similar names on a medical claim usually mean workers' compensation or auto liability, not health insurance.",
   "**BCBS cards**: the member ID begins with a **three-character alpha prefix** that identifies the member's home plan; claims are filed to the **local** Blue plan, which routes them through the **BlueCard** program to the home plan. Prefix **R** denotes the BCBS Federal Employee Program. Specific prefixes identify Medigap and state plans (the deck lists several New Jersey Horizon prefixes). A PPO suitcase logo on the card means BlueCard PPO benefits apply nationwide. **Aetna** IDs usually start with **W**; Aetna Medicare is a Medicare Advantage plan, billed to Aetna rather than Medicare. **UHC** cards show a member ID, a group number and the **payer ID 87726**; UHC also administers Medicare Advantage, Medicaid (UnitedHealthcare Community Plan) and Oxford plans with different addresses. **Cigna** cards carry the group number, the member ID and a payer ID 62308.",
   "**What to read on any card**: payer name and logo; plan type (HMO, PPO, POS, EPO, HDHP); member name and ID; group number; subscriber name if different; PCP name for HMO plans; copays by visit type; RxBIN/RxPCN for the pharmacy benefit; the **payer ID** for electronic claims and the claims mailing address; provider services and eligibility phone numbers; and any carve-out administrator for behavioural health, labs or imaging. Photograph both sides.",
   "Employer plans may be **fully insured** (the insurer takes the risk) or **self-funded** (the employer pays claims and the insurer only administers them as an ASO/TPA). Self-funded plans follow federal ERISA rules rather than state insurance law, which changes appeal rights and timelines, and the card often says \"administered by\"."
  ],
  compare:{head:["Payer","Card clue","Electronic payer ID"],rows:[["Blue Cross Blue Shield","3-letter alpha prefix; file to the local Blue plan","Varies by local plan"],["UnitedHealthcare","Member ID + group number; UHC logo","87726"],["Aetna","ID often starts with W","60054"],["Cigna","Group number + ID; Cigna tree logo","62308"],["Humana","Mostly Medicare Advantage cards","61101"],["BCBS Federal Employee Program","Prefix R","Local Blue plan"]]},
  terms:[["Commercial insurance","Private insurer coverage, individual or group."],["Alpha prefix","First three characters of a BCBS member ID identifying the home plan."],["BlueCard","BCBS program routing out-of-area claims between Blue plans."],["Payer ID","Code routing electronic claims to the right payer."],["Fully insured vs self-funded","Insurer carries the risk vs employer carries it with an administrator."],["ASO / TPA","Administrative services only / third-party administrator for a self-funded plan."],["ERISA","Federal law governing self-funded employer plans and their appeal rules."]],
  remember:"Bill the payer on the card for the DOS, at the payer ID on the card. A commercial name on the card does not mean a commercial product: it can be Medicare Advantage or Medicaid managed care underneath."},
 {id:"liability-wc",title:"Liability plans: workers' compensation and no-fault auto",
  line:"When an injury comes from work or an accident, a liability carrier pays before any health plan. These claims have their own forms, their own dates and no patient cost sharing.",
  body:[
   "**Liability insurance** protects a person or business from the financial consequences of injuries or losses caused in an accident. In healthcare billing two kinds matter.",
   "**Workers' compensation (WC)** ensures that medical bills and some lost wages are paid for employees injured on the job or who develop work-related illness, with benefits based on the type and severity of the injury. Features: multiple subscribers (the workers of a company), premium paid by the employer, **no dependents**, and the worker pays nothing: WC pays in full and the patient has no responsibility. Each state regulates WC with its own fee schedule and forms. Every WC claim needs the **Date of Injury (DOI)**, the employer, the WC carrier and the **claim or case number**; the accepted body part and diagnosis must match the injury; and treatment usually requires the carrier's authorisation. Services unrelated to the injury go to the patient's regular health plan.",
   "**No-fault or auto accident insurance** (personal injury protection, PIP, or med-pay) is a contract under which the insured is indemnified for losses by their own insurance company regardless of who caused the accident. In no-fault states the policyholder and passengers are paid by their own auto insurer and have limited rights to sue. Claims need the **date of accident**, the auto carrier, the claim number and the adjuster. **Third-party liability** (another party's insurer, a premises owner, a product manufacturer) may pay after a settlement, often through an attorney and a lien.",
   "Order of payment: workers' compensation or no-fault/liability pays first for the accident-related care; the health plan, including Medicare, is secondary and may make a **conditional payment** that it later recovers from the settlement. Medicare's MSP rules require reporting these cases. On the CMS-1500, box 10 (condition related to employment, auto accident, other accident) and box 14 (date of injury) must be completed."
  ],
  compare:{head:["","Workers' compensation","No-fault / auto (PIP)","Health plan"],rows:[["Pays for","Work-related injury or illness","Accident-related injury","Everything else"],["Key date","Date of injury (DOI)","Date of accident","Date of service"],["Patient cost sharing","None","Usually none up to the PIP limit","Copay, deductible, coinsurance"],["Dependents","None","Policyholder and passengers","Spouse and children"],["Order","Primary for the injury","Primary for the accident","Secondary / conditional"]]},
  terms:[["Liability insurance","Covers losses from accidents and injuries caused to others or to the insured."],["Workers' compensation","Employer-paid coverage for work injuries; no patient responsibility."],["Date of injury (DOI)","Date the employee was injured at work; required on WC claims."],["No-fault / PIP","Auto coverage paying the insured's own medical costs regardless of fault."],["Third-party liability","Another party's insurer responsible for the injury, often settled through an attorney."],["Conditional payment","Medicare paying while a liability case is open, to be repaid from the settlement."],["Subrogation","An insurer recovering what it paid from the party responsible."]],
  remember:"Accident or work injury means box 10 and box 14 on the claim and a liability carrier first. A health plan EOB denying \"other payer responsible\" is telling you exactly that."}
]}
];

/* Quiz questions for the RCM Basics course. `t` is the module short name. */
const BASICS_QUIZ=[
{t:"Working with the US",q:"A payer rep spells a member ID as “Whiskey, Bravo, 4, 5, 2, 1”. What did they say?",correct:"WB4521",wrongs:["VB4521","WD4521","MB4521"],ex:"NATO phonetic alphabet: Whiskey = W, Bravo = B. Read every ID back phonetically before the call ends."},
{t:"Working with the US",q:"A date of service is written <b>03/04/2026</b> on a US EOB. Which day is it?",correct:"4 March 2026",wrongs:["3 April 2026","4 March 2062","3 March 2026"],ex:"US dates are month/day/year. 03/04 is March 4th."},
{t:"Working with the US",q:"It is 7:00 pm in India in July. What time is it for a payer on the US East Coast?",correct:"About 9:30 am Eastern Daylight Time",wrongs:["About 6:30 am","About 11:30 pm the previous day","About 1:30 pm"],ex:"In summer (daylight saving) Eastern time is 9.5 hours behind IST; in winter it is 10.5 hours behind."},
{t:"Working with the US",q:"Which of these days will most US payer offices be <b>closed</b>?",correct:"The fourth Thursday of November (Thanksgiving)",wrongs:["26 January","15 August","The second Monday of December"],ex:"Thanksgiving is a federal holiday, usually with the Friday after taken too. Indian national holidays do not close US offices."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"Under HIPAA, which use of PHI needs <b>no</b> separate patient authorisation?",correct:"Sending the diagnosis on a claim to the payer (payment)",wrongs:["Emailing a patient’s chart to a relative who asks","Posting an identifiable patient case on social media","Selling patient lists to a marketing firm"],ex:"Treatment, payment and healthcare operations (TPO) are permitted uses. Everything else needs written authorisation."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"Which organisation is HIPAA <b>not</b> directly written for, but is bound through a business associate agreement?",correct:"A medical billing company working for providers",wrongs:["A hospital","A health insurance plan","A claims clearinghouse"],ex:"Covered entities are providers, health plans and clearinghouses. Vendors like billing companies are business associates."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"Which of these is PHI?",correct:"A spreadsheet of denied claims listing patient names and member IDs",wrongs:["A list of CPT codes and their fee schedule amounts","A count of denials by reason code with no patient data","A payer’s published timely filing policy"],ex:"Health information plus any of the 18 identifiers (name, member ID, DOB…) is PHI, whatever the format."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"The <b>minimum necessary</b> standard means:",correct:"Use or disclose only the PHI needed for the task at hand",wrongs:["Keep at least the minimum record retention period","Collect the minimum copay at every visit","Verify at least two identifiers before every call"],ex:"A payer call needs member ID, DOS and codes, not the whole chart."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"Which statement about the Affordable Care Act is correct?",correct:"Insurers cannot deny or limit coverage because of a pre-existing condition",wrongs:["It created Medicare Part D","It made Medicaid a federal-only program","It allows lifetime dollar limits on essential benefits"],ex:"The ACA (2010) brought guaranteed issue, no pre-existing exclusions, essential health benefits and no lifetime limits."},
{t:"Compliance",q:"A Marketplace patient’s eligibility shows coverage ‘pending premium payment’ in month two. What is happening?",correct:"The member is in the ACA 90-day grace period; claims may pend until the premium is paid",wrongs:["Coverage has been terminated retroactively","The plan is waiting for a prior authorisation","Medicaid has become primary"],ex:"Subsidised Marketplace members get a 90-day grace period. Verify again before writing the claim off."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"In the US healthcare flow, who submits the claim to the insurance company?",correct:"The provider or its medical billing organisation",wrongs:["The patient, after paying the doctor","The clearinghouse on its own initiative","The employer"],ex:"Unlike India, where the patient often claims reimbursement, the US provider bills the payer directly."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"Which of the 3Ps is a hospital laboratory?",correct:"Provider",wrongs:["Patient","Payer","Payee"],ex:"Any individual or organisation delivering medical services is a provider."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"A patient last saw this physician 4 years ago. For today’s visit the patient is:",correct:"A new patient",wrongs:["An established patient","An inpatient","A dependent"],ex:"New = no professional service from the physician or same-specialty group within 3 years."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"<b>Assignment of Benefits</b> is missing from the registration packet. What is the likely result?",correct:"The payer sends the payment to the patient instead of the provider",wrongs:["The claim rejects at the clearinghouse","The diagnosis cannot be released to the payer","The patient cannot be seen"],ex:"AOB directs payment to the provider. Without it, payment goes to the patient (the ‘claim paid to patient’ scenario)."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"Which provider <b>orders</b> an X-ray but does not perform it?",correct:"The ordering provider",wrongs:["The rendering provider","The attending provider","The billing provider"],ex:"Ordering provider orders tests; rendering provider performs the service; attending is responsible during an admission."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"Place of service code <b>23</b> is:",correct:"Emergency room, hospital",wrongs:["Office","Inpatient hospital","Ambulatory surgical center"],ex:"11 office, 21 inpatient, 22 outpatient hospital, 23 ER, 24 ASC, 12 home, 34 hospice."},
{t:"Healthcare & 3Ps",q:"A Skilled Nursing Facility must have, among other things:",correct:"Nursing care available 24 hours a day and at least one full-time registered nurse",wrongs:["An operating room for same-day surgery","A hospice election form for every resident","Only physicians on staff"],ex:"SNFs meet federal criteria for Medicare/Medicaid nursing care: physician supervision, full-time RN, records, 24-hour nursing."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"Who is the <b>subscriber</b>?",correct:"The person who holds the policy, often the employee",wrongs:["Any family member covered by the policy","The provider who accepts the plan","The payer’s claims representative"],ex:"Subscriber holds the policy; dependents are covered under it; both are beneficiaries."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"Which type of coverage has <b>no dependents</b>?",correct:"Workers’ compensation",wrongs:["Employer group health insurance","Individual family plan","Medicaid"],ex:"WC covers the injured worker only, premium paid by the employer, no patient cost sharing."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"Billed $900, allowed $600, deductible remaining $200, 80:20 coinsurance, in network. What does the patient owe?",correct:"$280",wrongs:["$380","$80","$500"],ex:"Deductible $200, then 20% of the remaining $400 = $80. Patient $280, payer $320, contractual adjustment $300."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"What is an <b>out-of-pocket maximum</b>?",correct:"The most the patient pays in a plan year for covered in-network care before the plan pays 100%",wrongs:["The maximum the plan pays for a benefit per year","The provider’s highest charge for a service","The monthly premium limit"],ex:"Deductible, coinsurance and copays count toward it; premiums do not."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"A plan covers 20 physical therapy visits per year. Visit 21 will:",correct:"Deny as benefit maximum reached and become patient responsibility unless an extension was authorised",wrongs:["Pay at 50%","Be covered by the secondary automatically","Be written off as a contractual adjustment"],ex:"Visit limits and benefit maximums shift the cost to the patient; verify them during eligibility for therapy, chiropractic, dental and vision."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"A patient needs a referral from a PCP to see a specialist and has no out-of-network benefits. Which plan type?",correct:"HMO",wrongs:["PPO","EPO","Indemnity"],ex:"HMO: PCP and referrals required, in-network only. PPO: neither required, OON covered. EPO: no referrals but in-network only."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"Referral versus prior authorisation:",correct:"A referral is the PCP’s permission to see a specialist; a prior authorisation is the payer’s advance approval for a service",wrongs:["They are the same document","A referral comes from the payer; an authorisation from the PCP","Only PPO plans use referrals"],ex:"HMO and POS plans need referrals; any plan may require prior authorisation for specific services."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"A child is covered by both parents. Mother born 12 May, father born 3 February. Which plan is primary?",correct:"The father’s plan, because his birthday comes first in the calendar year",wrongs:["The mother’s plan, because she is younger","The plan with the lower deductible","Whichever plan the parents choose"],ex:"Birthday rule: month and day only, not the year."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"A patient is the subscriber on Plan X and a dependent on spouse’s Plan Y. Which is primary for the patient?",correct:"Plan X, where the patient is the subscriber",wrongs:["Plan Y, because dependent plans pay first","Both pay 50%","The plan that has existed longer"],ex:"The plan covering the person as the employee/subscriber is primary over the one covering them as a dependent."},
{t:"Insurance basics",q:"For a par (in-network) provider, the difference between billed and allowed is:",correct:"A contractual adjustment, written off and never billed to the patient",wrongs:["Balance billed to the patient","Billed to the secondary payer","Refunded by the payer"],ex:"CO-45 contractual obligation. Only non-par providers may balance bill, where the law allows."},
{t:"Revenue cycle",q:"Which step comes immediately <b>after</b> medical coding in the RCM cycle?",correct:"Demographic and charge entry",wrongs:["Claim submission","Payment posting","Eligibility verification"],ex:"Encounter → coding → charge entry → claim submission → clearinghouse → adjudication → posting → AR."},
{t:"Revenue cycle",q:"Which phase of the revenue cycle produces the most denials?",correct:"The front end: scheduling, registration and eligibility",wrongs:["Payment posting","Credit balance","Patient collections"],ex:"Wrong member IDs, missed authorisations and unknown secondary plans are created at the front end and surface as denials weeks later."},
{t:"Revenue cycle",q:"What does the clearinghouse send back after scrubbing a claim file?",correct:"A report of claims received, rejected with reasons, and forwarded to payers",wrongs:["The EOB","The patient statement","The prior authorisation number"],ex:"The scrubber report must be worked daily; a rejected claim never reached the payer."},
{t:"Revenue cycle",q:"<b>Days in AR</b> measures:",correct:"How long, on average, a charge waits before it is paid",wrongs:["How many claims were denied","How many days the office is open","The payer’s timely filing limit"],ex:"Total AR ÷ average daily charges. Lower is better; under 40 days is a common target."},
{t:"Front end",q:"During pre-registration the front desk should capture:",correct:"Demographics, insurance card details, guarantor and the reason for the visit",wrongs:["The CPT codes for the visit","The allowed amount","The ERA"],ex:"Pre-registration feeds eligibility verification and the claim; codes come later from the encounter."},
{t:"Front end",q:"Which electronic transaction checks eligibility?",correct:"270 inquiry / 271 response",wrongs:["837","835","276/277"],ex:"270/271 eligibility, 276/277 claim status, 837 claim, 835 remittance, 278 authorisation."},
{t:"Front end",q:"Eligibility should be verified for:",correct:"The exact date of service, the rendering provider and the location",wrongs:["Today’s date only","The date the claim will be filed","The subscriber’s hire date"],ex:"Coverage can start or end on any day; Medicaid can change monthly. Verify for the DOS."},
{t:"Front end",q:"The <b>facesheet</b> is:",correct:"The registration form with demographics, insurance details and the AOB/ROI signatures",wrongs:["The first page of the EOB","The clearinghouse rejection report","The coded superbill"],ex:"Every facesheet field maps to a claim box."},
{t:"Front end",q:"The medical record shows a procedure that is not on the superbill. The coder should:",correct:"Code from the documentation, after confirming with the provider if needed",wrongs:["Bill only what is circled on the superbill","Bill both and let the payer decide","Delete the note"],ex:"Codes come from the documented encounter. The superbill is a summary, not the source."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"In ICD-10-CM code <b>S42.321A</b>, what does the final character A mean?",correct:"Initial encounter",wrongs:["Left arm","Adult patient","Acute fracture"],ex:"Seventh character: A initial, D subsequent, S sequela."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"How are ICD-10-CM codes structured?",correct:"3 to 7 characters; first is a letter, second and third are digits, decimal after the third",wrongs:["Always 5 numeric digits","A letter plus 4 digits","4 digits followed by F or T"],ex:"S42.321A: letter, two digits, decimal, up to four more characters."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"A code written as <b>4 digits followed by T</b> is:",correct:"A CPT Category III code for emerging technology",wrongs:["A CPT Category II performance code","A HCPCS Level II supply code","An ICD-10-PCS code"],ex:"Category II = 4 digits + F (performance, no payment). Category III = 4 digits + T (emerging technology)."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"Which code is a <b>HCPCS Level II</b> code?",correct:"J1885",wrongs:["99214","3074F","S42.321A"],ex:"HCPCS Level II = letter + 4 digits (A, E, G, J, L codes…) for drugs, DME, supplies and Medicare services."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"Which modifier reports the <b>professional component</b> of a radiology service?",correct:"26",wrongs:["TC","50","59"],ex:"26 = professional (reading), TC = technical (equipment/staff). No modifier = global."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"Two different physicians performed the same chest X-ray on the same patient on the same day. The second claim needs:",correct:"Modifier 77 (repeat procedure by another provider)",wrongs:["Modifier 76","Modifier 25","Modifier 50"],ex:"76 same provider, 77 different provider. Without it the second claim denies as a duplicate."},
{t:"Coding & charge entry",q:"At charge entry the patient should be located in the system by:",correct:"Name and date of birth together",wrongs:["Name alone","Phone number","Insurance company name"],ex:"Posting to the wrong patient is both a billing error and a HIPAA breach."},
{t:"Claims & adjudication",q:"A hospital’s facility charges for an inpatient stay go on:",correct:"The UB-04 (837I)",wrongs:["The CMS-1500 (837P)","The ADA dental form","The patient statement"],ex:"Institutional = UB-04 with revenue codes and a type of bill; professional = CMS-1500."},
{t:"Claims & adjudication",q:"Rejection versus denial:",correct:"A rejection never entered the payer’s system; a denial was adjudicated and refused",wrongs:["They are the same thing","A rejection comes from the payer; a denial from the clearinghouse","A denial can be fixed and resent as a brand-new claim; a rejection must be appealed"],ex:"Rejections have no claim number and do not count toward timely filing; denials carry a claim number and reason codes."},
{t:"Claims & adjudication",q:"The electronic, machine-readable version of the EOB is the:",correct:"835 ERA",wrongs:["837P","270","UB-04"],ex:"835 Electronic Remittance Advice posts automatically; the EOB is the paper or PDF statement."},
{t:"Claims & adjudication",q:"A claim was sent 45 days ago and the payer has no record of it. Its status is:",correct:"No response: resubmit with proof of timely filing",wrongs:["Paid","Partially paid","Credit balance"],ex:"No-response claims are worked after normal processing time; the clearinghouse acceptance report proves the original filing date."},
{t:"Claims & adjudication",q:"On a remittance, group code <b>CO</b> with reason 45 means:",correct:"The contractual difference between billed and allowed; the provider absorbs it",wrongs:["The patient owes the amount","The claim was a duplicate","The payer needs medical records"],ex:"CO-45 is the contractual adjustment. PR codes (1 deductible, 2 coinsurance, 3 copay) are the patient’s."},
{t:"Payments & AR",q:"Posting an insurance payment requires:",correct:"The EOB or ERA plus the cheque or EFT details",wrongs:["Only the patient statement","Only the superbill","The prior authorisation letter"],ex:"The remittance tells you how to split allowed, adjustments and patient responsibility; the deposit tells you what to reconcile against."},
{t:"Payments & AR",q:"After posting a batch, the posted total must equal:",correct:"The cheque or EFT deposit amount, to the cent",wrongs:["The total billed charges","The total allowed amount","The previous day’s batch"],ex:"Batch reconciliation. Differences are unapplied cash to be researched, never ignored."},
{t:"Payments & AR",q:"An account shows a negative balance because two payers each paid as primary. This is:",correct:"A credit balance caused by a COB conflict; one payer must be refunded",wrongs:["A contractual adjustment","Bad debt","Patient responsibility"],ex:"Credit balances come from overpayments, duplicate payments and COB conflicts. Medicare overpayments must be returned within 60 days of identification."},
{t:"Payments & AR",q:"Before sending the first patient statement you should confirm:",correct:"Insurance has paid correctly and the balance is truly the patient’s",wrongs:["The patient’s credit score","The collection agency’s fee","The payer’s timely filing limit"],ex:"Billing a patient for a payer’s error loses the money and the patient."},
{t:"Payments & AR",q:"A <b>dunning letter</b> is:",correct:"A demand letter in a collection sequence for an unpaid patient balance",wrongs:["A payer’s records request","The clearinghouse rejection notice","An appeal cover letter"],ex:"Statements, then dunning letters, then a collection agency referral and bad-debt write-off."},
{t:"US payers",q:"Which Medicare part covers a physician’s office visit?",correct:"Part B",wrongs:["Part A","Part C only","Part D"],ex:"A inpatient/SNF/hospice/home health; B physician, outpatient, DME, preventive; C Medicare Advantage; D drugs."},
{t:"US payers",q:"A Medicare Beneficiary Identifier looks like:",correct:"11 characters mixing letters and numbers, e.g. 1EG4-TE5-MK73",wrongs:["A 9-digit SSN followed by a letter","3 letters then 9 digits","A 10-digit number starting with 1"],ex:"The MBI replaced the SSN-based HICN in 2020. The old format is no longer accepted."},
{t:"US payers",q:"Medicare Part B pays what share of the fee schedule amount after the deductible?",correct:"80%, with the patient owing 20%",wrongs:["100%","50%","90%"],ex:"The 20% is why a Medigap or secondary plan matters to Medicare patients."},
{t:"US payers",q:"A patient has Medicare and Medicaid. Which is primary?",correct:"Medicare",wrongs:["Medicaid","Whichever card is presented first","Both pay equally"],ex:"Medicaid is always the payer of last resort."},
{t:"US payers",q:"Which statement about Medicaid is correct?",correct:"It is administered by each state within federal rules and eligibility can change monthly",wrongs:["Its rules are identical in every state","It is only for people over 65","Providers may balance bill Medicaid patients"],ex:"State-run, verify monthly, never balance bill, always last payer."},
{t:"US payers",q:"TRICARE claims for a patient in Texas go to:",correct:"TriWest Healthcare Alliance (TRICARE West)",wrongs:["Health Net Federal Services","Humana Military (TRICARE East)","The local Medicare contractor"],ex:"Since 1 January 2025 TRICARE has two regions: East (Humana Military) and West (TriWest). Texas moved to West."},
{t:"US payers",q:"<b>CHAMPVA</b> covers:",correct:"Spouses and children of veterans who are permanently disabled from, or died of, a service-connected condition",wrongs:["Active-duty military members","All US veterans","Federal civilian employees"],ex:"CHAMPVA is a VA program for dependents; it is not TRICARE and is secondary to Medicare."},
{t:"US payers",q:"A BCBS member ID begins with three letters. Those letters are:",correct:"The alpha prefix identifying the member’s home Blue plan",wrongs:["The group number","The patient’s initials","The plan type"],ex:"File to the local Blue plan, which routes via BlueCard to the home plan. Prefix R = Federal Employee Program."},
{t:"US payers",q:"A card says ‘Aetna Medicare’. The claim goes to:",correct:"Aetna, because it is a Medicare Advantage plan",wrongs:["The regional Medicare contractor","Medicaid","Both Aetna and Medicare"],ex:"Medicare Advantage (Part C) plans replace Original Medicare for their members; Medicare itself will deny the claim."},
{t:"US payers",q:"An employee is injured at work. The claim for that injury goes first to:",correct:"The employer’s workers’ compensation carrier",wrongs:["The employee’s health plan","Medicare","The employee as self-pay"],ex:"Liability coverage (WC, no-fault) pays before any health plan. Complete box 10 and box 14 with the date of injury."},
{t:"US payers",q:"PACE participants’ prescription drugs are paid by:",correct:"The PACE organisation, which covers all of the participant’s care",wrongs:["A separate Medicare Part D plan the patient must join","Medicaid only","The patient"],ex:"PACE receives a capitated payment and provides all care, including Part D drugs. Joining a separate Part D plan disenrols the patient from PACE."}
];

